// InlineLeaves — the Std→Std inliner. Two admission rules, one splice:
//
//   • THE LEAF RULE: a direct call to a TINY LEAF function — no call of any kind, inside
//     `MaxInlinedLeafOps` — is replaced at EVERY site by a copy of the body. The call is nearly all of such
//     a function's cost: MEASURED on the self-compile, `regMaskContains` — `((mask shr regNum) and 1) != 0`
//     plus its entry guard — was 21% of the profile's self time from 14 static sites, because the body is
//     smaller than the protocol around it (the argument moves, the `call`, the frame, the `ret`).
//   • THE CALLED-ONCE RULE: a function with exactly one direct `call` site in the whole program is spliced
//     into its caller whatever its body holds — loops, stores, panics, `div`/`mod`, calls of every kind.
//     The call was the function's only reason to exist as a frame, and the body moves rather than
//     multiplies. Callees are spliced before their callers (post-order over the direct-call graph), so a
//     chain nests naturally and a spliced body's own site table is remapped into its caller's.
//
// ⭐⭐ **A SPLICED BODY KEEPS ITS FRAME IN EVERY STACK TRACE.** Every block a splice copies carries the
// splice's INLINE SITE (`IrBlock.inlineSite`: the callee's name and the site it nests under); the backends
// emit one code range per run of blocks under a site (`BacktraceFormat`'s `__inlframes`), and the frame
// printers name the sites a return address or a faulting instruction lies under ahead of the frame's own
// function. That is what lets a panic block be COPIED like any other block: a panic raised inside a spliced
// body prints `in <callee>` then `in <caller>`, at every nesting level, exactly as the call did.
//
// ONE ROUND: the called-once rule is applied to each callee once, in waves that splice a callee only after
// every called-once callee inside it; the leaf rule then decides eligibility on the bodies as they stand and
// splices every admitted site. Neither cascades further.
//
// Three things a general Std-tier inliner needs and this one does not:
//   • **an ownership side table** — refcounts are inserted at the MAXON tier, so a retain, a release
//     and a scope drop are all `call` ops, copied verbatim like any other call. There is nothing to
//     thread.
//   • **cross-module snapshots** — `stdlib/` is compiled into the same module. There is nothing to
//     snapshot.
//   • **a slot-op refusal** — `stackRecordAddr` is `isUnsupportedInInlineBody`, and
//     `promoteStackRecords` runs AFTER this pass, so none exists yet either way.
//
// `BlockArgReturnRole` is declared and nothing reads it; wiring it here would be a producer with no
// consumer.
//
// ⚠ **THE PARAMETER HALF OF THE REMAP COLUMN IS A RANGE, NOT A SEARCH.** `ValueId`s `0..paramCount-1`
// ARE the parameters by the Std ABI's own convention (`IrValueId.maxon`, and
// `InsertRangeChecks.rangedParamValue` rests on the same identity), and the `param` ops the copy skips
// are CHECKED against that convention as they go by (`assertParamOpMatchesConvention`). A scan that
// stopped at the first non-param op would bind nothing when anything is prepended, and every spliced
// body op would then read the CALLER's same-numbered value.

// ⭐ **THE OP BUDGET, AND WHAT IT ADMITS — MEASURED, at this tier, not guessed from the target dump.**
// Counted over all of a callee's blocks with terminators included and `param` ops excluded (a `param`
// becomes the call's argument rather than an instruction).
//
// ⭐ **WHAT HOLDS 24 UP IS THE SITE COUNT.** MEASURED on the self-compile: at 24, 3,673 sites inline
// across 418 eligible callees; at 16, 3,286 sites across 349 callees — 24 buys 387 sites and 69 callees
// over 16, a tenth more inlining. Lowering it is a change in its own right and owes its own CPU A/B. The
// pass reports what `ReportedLargerLeafBudget` would additionally admit, so the next person to ask has
// the number for THIS compile instead of a guess.
let MaxInlinedLeafOps = 24 as OpCount

// The second budget the pass MEASURES but does not use — reported beside the refusals so that "would a
// bigger one be better?" is answered from the compile being asked about rather than re-derived. It is
// deliberately not a knob: two budgets that both took effect would be two rules.
let ReportedLargerLeafBudget = 32 as OpCount

// The block labels this pass mints. They reach the committed goldens, so they are short and prefixed
// `__il_` (inline-leaves — disjoint from `InsertRangeChecks`' `__rc_` and `InlineManagedPrimitives`'
// `__im_`).
//
// ⭐ **A COPIED block KEEPS THE CALLEE BLOCK'S OWN LABEL — except the ENTRY, and that exception is the
// rule.** `__rc_ok`, `ifcont`, `whilehdr` are claims about a CONSTRUCT, and every one of them is still
// true after the block is copied into another function; `entry` is a claim about a FUNCTION, and a copied
// block is not the caller's entry. Copied unchanged it would put a second block called `entry` inside the
// caller — printed as `entry#0` beside the real one — which is exactly the label a reader trusts not to
// be ambiguous.
let InlineContLabel = b"__il_cont"
let InlineBodyLabel = b"__il_body"

typealias SiteCount = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias SiteCountArray = Array with SiteCount

// How many parameter cascades one callee carries — its arity at most, and one in nearly every case.
typealias GuardCount = int(0 to u64.max)

// The shared empties a `SiteSplicer` starts with and NEVER writes to — `seedSiteBuffers` replaces them
// with real arrays at the first site, and `valueIds` replaces the minter at the first splice. The leaf
// rule constructs one inliner per FUNCTION in the module and the overwhelming majority of functions hold
// no inlinable site at all, so four `create()`s in the constructor would be four heap objects per
// function for nothing. Same shape, and the same measured reason, as
// `InlineManagedPrimitives.sharedUnseededMinter` and `InsertRangeChecks`' chain columns; safe for the
// same reason — the one operation that would write to each is the one that replaces it.
let sharedEmptySitePositions = BlockOpPosArray.create()
let sharedEmptySiteCallees = FuncIndexArray.create()
let sharedEmptyContPositions = BlockPositionColumn.create()
let sharedUnseededLeafMinter = ValueMinter.create(0)

// ⭐ The two columns a splice that declines NOTHING never writes to — which is nearly every splice in the
// module, since a callee with a narrowed parameter is a handful of them. Shared for the three empties
// above's reason and with their invariant: `DeclinedGuards.decline` REPLACES both fields before its first
// write, so neither is ever mutated. Fresh columns would be two allocations per splice for a plan almost
// every splice answers `false` from.
let sharedEmptyDeclineColumn = BoolArray.create()
let sharedEmptyForwardColumn = BlockIdArray.create()

// --- what ONE op of a candidate body means to the two rules ---
enum InlineOpRole
	// Copyable verbatim into the caller.
	body

	// A `param`: not counted against the leaf budget and never copied — the splice maps its value to the
	// call site's argument instead.
	parameter

	// A call of ANY kind. The leaf rule refuses it — which is what makes ownership free there (a
	// retain/release/scope-drop is a `call`) — and the called-once rule copies it.
	calling

	// A body op whose diagnostic names a FRAME: a panic op, or a divide that can fault. Copied like any
	// other op where the target emits inline frame records; refused by the leaf rule where it does not.
	framed

	// An op the dialect itself marks unsupported inside an inline body. See `inlineOpRole`.
	refusing
end 'InlineOpRole'

// ⭐⭐ **THE TWO METADATA BITS THIS PASS ASKS PER OP, MEMOIZED PER VARIANT.** `rawValue` constructs a
// variant's backing struct by one compare per VARIANT of the union — ninety-odd for `StdOp` — so read per
// op it costs several times the jump-table `match` it sits behind; `ordinal` is the discriminant itself.
// Each variant's metadata is read from `rawValue` the first time the variant is met and answered from a
// column after, so the dialect's table stays the one source and the walk pays one probe per op.
typealias OpRoleColumn = Array with OpRole

type StdOpMetaMemo
	var known as BoolArray
	var roles as OpRoleColumn
	var unsupported as BoolArray

	static function create() returns StdOpMetaMemo
		return Self{known: BoolArray.create(), roles: OpRoleColumn.create(), unsupported: BoolArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	function hasRole(op StdOp, role OpRole) returns bool
		let variant = self.learned(op)
		return (try self.roles.get(variant) otherwise panic("StdOpMetaMemo.hasRole: roles.get OOB at {variant} after learning it")) == role
	end 'hasRole'

	function isUnsupportedInInlineBody(op StdOp) returns bool
		let variant = self.learned(op)
		return try self.unsupported.get(variant) otherwise panic("StdOpMetaMemo.isUnsupportedInInlineBody: unsupported.get OOB at {variant} after learning it")
	end 'isUnsupportedInInlineBody'

	// The op's variant ordinal, its metadata filled in on the first meeting.
	function learned(op StdOp) returns ElementIndex
		let variant = op.ordinal as ElementIndex
		self.known.growFilled(variant + 1, value: false)

		let met = try self.known.get(variant) otherwise panic("StdOpMetaMemo.learned: known.get OOB at {variant} after growing to hold it")

		if met 'alreadyLearned'
			return variant
		end 'alreadyLearned'

		// `growFilled` writes every slot it adds, which is what gives an enum column storage (`resize` on
		// one sets the length alone — `CompileTimings.create` records the trap). The backing record is read
		// one field at a time, which is the only way the compiler serves it.
		self.roles.growFilled(variant + 1, value: OpRole.plain)
		self.unsupported.growFilled(variant + 1, value: false)
		try self.roles.set(variant, value: op.rawValue.role) otherwise panic("StdOpMetaMemo.learned: roles.set OOB at {variant}")
		try self.unsupported.set(variant, value: op.rawValue.isUnsupportedInInlineBody) otherwise panic("StdOpMetaMemo.learned: unsupported.set OOB at {variant}")
		try self.known.set(variant, value: true) otherwise panic("StdOpMetaMemo.learned: known.set OOB at {variant}")
		return variant
	end 'learned'
end 'StdOpMetaMemo'

// ⭐ **THE ROLE OF ONE OP — one exhaustive `match`, and the TAIL of it reads the dialect's own flag.**
// The variants named explicitly are the ones whose role is NOT derivable from
// `isUnsupportedInInlineBody`: the five calls and `param` all carry `false` for it (a call is perfectly
// representable inside a body — it just is not a LEAF's body), the two panics carry `true` while being
// copied like any other op where a copied panic block keeps its frame through the block's inline site,
// and `div`/`mod` carry `false` while `idiv` faults on `i64.min / -1` — the fault handler symbolizes the
// faulting address through the same inline ranges a panic's return address goes through
// (`safety/integer-overflow-fault-from-int-min-over-minus-one` and `inline-called-once`'s hardware fault
// case pin the two shapes). Those four are `framed`, the role a target without inline frame records
// refuses. Everything else answers off the flag, which is what gives that field its first reader and
// means an op added with it set is refused here without this file learning its name.
//
// ⚠ Every other variant is NAMED, so a variant added later fails to compile here until it is
// consciously placed.
function inlineOpRole(op StdOp, meta StdOpMetaMemo) returns InlineOpRole
	match op 'kind'
		const or
			binOp or
			unaryOp or
			cmp or
			binOpImm or
			cmpImm then break 'kind'
		div or
			mod then return InlineOpRole.framed
		condBranch or
			branch or
			ret then break 'kind'
		param then return InlineOpRole.parameter
		call then return InlineOpRole.calling
		retVoid or
			globalAddr or
			loadIndirect or
			storeIndirect or
			osAllocPages or
			osWriteStdout or
			rdataAddr then break 'kind'
		tryCall then return InlineOpRole.calling
		errorReturn or
			osExit or
			funcAddr then break 'kind'
		callIndirect then return InlineOpRole.calling
		osPanic then return InlineOpRole.framed
		osReadClock or
			osProcessSpawn or
			osProcessExitCode or
			osWaitHandles or
			osCloseHandle or
			osWaitHandle or
			osFreePages or
			switch then break 'kind'
		witnessCall then return InlineOpRole.calling
		osPipeCreate or
			osIocpCreate or
			osEventCreate or
			osThreadCreate or
			osLockInit or
			osLockEnter or
			osLockLeave or
			osReadOverlapped or
			osLastError or
			osProcessSpawnInherit or
			osCancelIo or
			osGetPid or
			osPipeCreateOutbound or
			osWriteFile or
			osReadWallClock then break 'kind'
		witnessTryCall then return InlineOpRole.calling
		osFileOpenRead or
			osFileClose or
			osReadFile or
			osFileOpenWrite or
			osFileSize or
			osFileDelete or
			osFileRename or
			osFileFindFirst or
			osFileFindClose or
			osFileFindNext or
			osDirCreate or
			osDirCurrentPath or
			osFileAttributes then break 'kind'
		osPanicValue then return InlineOpRole.framed

		// The allocator's page/fill/atomic band and the scheduler's thread/TLS band (F1, F2, G1) are placed
		// here consciously: none is a call site, a terminator or a `param`, so each one's own
		// `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` answers below — a fill and the two atomics are body ops a body may
		// hold, the import calls refuse.
		osCommandLine or
			osStdHandle or
			osExecutablePath or
			osWriteStderr or
			stackRecordAddr or
			osOpenNamedSegment or
			osMapNamedSegment or
			osTickCountMs or
			osHandleSetInherit or
			osPipePeek or
			osEnvRead or
			osProcessTerminate or
			osSocketStartup or
			osSocketResolve or
			osSocketFreeAddrInfo or
			osSocketCreate or
			osSocketConnect or
			osSocketSend or
			osSocketRecv or
			osSocketClose or
			osCpuCount or
			osThreadCpuTicks or
			osDecommitPages or
			memFill or
			osCommitPages or
			osReservePages or
			atomicCas or
			atomicRmw or
			currentThreadToken or
			tlsAlloc or
			tlsSetValue or
			tlsSlotLoad or
			osSetEvent or
			osEnterBackgroundPriority then break 'kind'
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable then break 'kind'
		osEnvBlock or
			osEnvBlockFree then break 'kind'
		loadAcquire or
			storeRelease then break 'kind'
		osHandleFileType then break 'kind'
		osCreateNamedSegment or
			osUnmapNamedSegment or
			osUnpublishNamedSegment or
			covPoint or
			osHostSample or
			osNetpoll then break 'kind'
	end 'kind'

	return InlineOpRole.refusing if meta.isUnsupportedInInlineBody(op) else InlineOpRole.body
end 'inlineOpRole'

// --- the per-callee verdict ---
enum LeafVerdict
	// The body may be spliced at any direct call site that is not its own function.
	eligible

	// Inside every other rule but past `MaxInlinedLeafOps`. Kept apart from `notALeaf` because it is the
	// only refusal a BUDGET could change, and the report says how many sites it costs.
	tooManyOps

	// Everything else, and the list is the whole eligibility rule read backwards: a call of any kind, an
	// `isUnsupportedInInlineBody` op, a `framed` op on a target without inline frame records, a
	// by-reference parameter, a body that carries the green-thread stack guard, an entry block carrying
	// phis, a block still in `Terminator.unset` (a body the lowering never emitted), or no body at all.
	notALeaf
end 'LeafVerdict'

typealias LeafVerdictArray = Array with LeafVerdict

// What one scan of a candidate body found. A record rather than two out-params because the verdict is a
// JOIN of several facts and the budget question needs the op count even when the answer is `eligible`.
type LeafBodyScan
	export var verdict as LeafVerdict
	export var opCount as OpCount

	export static function create(verdict LeafVerdict, opCount OpCount) returns LeafBodyScan
		return Self{verdict: verdict, opCount: opCount}
	end 'create'
end 'LeafBodyScan'

// ⭐ **EVERY FUNCTION'S VERDICT, COMPUTED ONCE, ON THE PRE-SPLICE BODY.** Recomputing spliceability per
// SITE is `callSites × calleeSize`; here the scan is one walk of the module and every site is a column
// read.
//
// ⭐⭐ **PRE-SPLICE IS ALSO THE SEMANTICS, NOT MERELY THE SCHEDULE.** Because the verdicts are all taken
// before the first splice, a caller that BECOMES call-free by being inlined into does not become a leaf
// in this compile. That is the "one round, no cascade" rule, and it is what bounds the work at one copy
// per site — stated here rather than left to be inferred from the order of two loops.
type LeafPlan
	var verdicts as LeafVerdictArray

	// One past the highest ValueId each ELIGIBLE callee's body uses — the width of the value column a
	// splice builds — and the block count it was admitted with. Zero for every other function;
	// `nextFreeValueId` walks the body, so it is asked only where an answer will be spent.
	var valueBounds as ValueIdArray
	var blockCounts as BlockPositionColumn

	// The same membership as `verdicts == eligible`, in the shape `ValueOriginTable.indexRowsOfFunctions`
	// takes. Built in the same walk rather than derived later, so the two cannot disagree about which
	// callees' origin rows are indexed.
	export var eligibleFuncs as BoolArray

	// `verdicts == tooManyOps` AND inside `ReportedLargerLeafBudget` — a BIT rather than the op count it
	// is derived from, because the count has exactly one reader and this is what that reader asks.
	var withinLargerBudget as BoolArray
	export var eligibleCount as FuncIndex
	export var tooManyOpsCount as FuncIndex
	export var tooManyOpsWithinLargerBudget as FuncIndex

	static function create(funcCount FuncIndex) returns LeafPlan
		var valueBounds = ValueIdArray.create()
		valueBounds.resize(funcCount)
		var blockCounts = BlockPositionColumn.create()
		blockCounts.resize(funcCount)
		var eligibleFuncs = BoolArray.create()
		eligibleFuncs.resize(funcCount)
		var withinLargerBudget = BoolArray.create()
		withinLargerBudget.resize(funcCount)
		return Self{
			verdicts: LeafVerdictArray.create(),
			valueBounds: valueBounds,
			blockCounts: blockCounts,
			eligibleFuncs: eligibleFuncs,
			withinLargerBudget: withinLargerBudget,
			eligibleCount: 0,
			tooManyOpsCount: 0,
			tooManyOpsWithinLargerBudget: 0
		}
	end 'create'

	// File function `funcIndex`'s verdict. Called once per function, in index order, so `verdicts` is
	// PUSHED — an `Array with <enum>` is a struct array, and `resize` sets such an array's length without
	// giving its slots storage (`CompileTimings.create` records that trap for `Array with PhaseCost`).
	function record(funcIndex FuncIndex, scan LeafBodyScan, module StdModule, func IrFunction)
		if funcIndex != self.verdicts.count() 'outOfOrder'
			panic("LeafPlan.record: asked for function {funcIndex} with {self.verdicts.count()} already recorded — the verdict column is pushed in index order, so it IS the function numbering")
		end 'outOfOrder'

		self.verdicts.push(scan.verdict)

		match scan.verdict 'tally'
			eligible then self.recordEligible(funcIndex, module: module, func: func)
			tooManyOps then self.recordTooManyOps(funcIndex, opCount: scan.opCount)
			notALeaf then break 'tally'
		end 'tally'
	end 'record'

	function recordEligible(funcIndex FuncIndex, module StdModule, func IrFunction)
		self.eligibleCount = self.eligibleCount + 1
		try self.eligibleFuncs.set(funcIndex, value: true) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.recordEligible: eligibleFuncs.set OOB at {funcIndex}")
		try self.valueBounds.set(funcIndex, value: nextFreeValueId(module, func: func)) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.recordEligible: valueBounds.set OOB at {funcIndex}")
		try self.blockCounts.set(funcIndex, value: func.blockRefs.count()) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.recordEligible: blockCounts.set OOB at {funcIndex}")
	end 'recordEligible'

	function recordTooManyOps(funcIndex FuncIndex, opCount OpCount)
		self.tooManyOpsCount = self.tooManyOpsCount + 1

		if opCount > ReportedLargerLeafBudget 'pastEvenTheLargerBudget'
			return
		end 'pastEvenTheLargerBudget'

		self.tooManyOpsWithinLargerBudget = self.tooManyOpsWithinLargerBudget + 1
		try self.withinLargerBudget.set(funcIndex, value: true) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.recordTooManyOps: withinLargerBudget.set OOB at {funcIndex}")
	end 'recordTooManyOps'

	export function verdictOf(funcIndex FuncIndex) returns LeafVerdict
		return try self.verdicts.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.verdictOf: function index {funcIndex} is outside the {self.verdicts.count()}-function module this plan was built over")
	end 'verdictOf'

	export function isWithinLargerBudget(funcIndex FuncIndex) returns bool
		return try self.withinLargerBudget.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.isWithinLargerBudget: function index {funcIndex} is outside the {self.withinLargerBudget.count()}-function module this plan was built over")
	end 'isWithinLargerBudget'

	export function valueBoundOf(funcIndex FuncIndex) returns ValueId
		return try self.valueBounds.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.valueBoundOf: function index {funcIndex} is outside the {self.valueBounds.count()}-function module this plan was built over")
	end 'valueBoundOf'

	export function blockCountOf(funcIndex FuncIndex) returns BlockRefIndex
		return try self.blockCounts.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("LeafPlan.blockCountOf: function index {funcIndex} is outside the {self.blockCounts.count()}-function module this plan was built over")
	end 'blockCountOf'
end 'LeafPlan'

function buildLeafPlan(module StdModule, framesAreRecorded bool, namedByGolden BoolArray, meta StdOpMetaMemo) returns LeafPlan
	var plan = LeafPlan.create(module.funcCount() as FuncIndex)

	for funcIndex in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		let func = module.getFunc(funcIndex)

		// Splicing every site of a golden-named leaf would drop the body the golden asks the printer for.
		let scan = LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: 0) if isGoldenNamed(namedByGolden, funcIndex: funcIndex) else scanLeafBody(module, func: func, framesAreRecorded: framesAreRecorded, meta: meta)
		plan.record(funcIndex, scan: scan, module: module, func: func)
	end 'eachFunc'

	return plan
end 'buildLeafPlan'

// One candidate body's whole verdict, from ONE walk: the shape both rules refuse, then the leaf rule's
// own — a call of any kind, a `framed` op where the target records no inline frames, and the op budget.
//
// ⚠ **IT WALKS `blockRefs` → `opRefs` + terminator DIRECTLY rather than `IrModule.liveOpIndices`, and
// that is the same descent** — but the array version MATERIALIZES the index list, and this runs over
// EVERY function in the module including the large majority that answer `notALeaf` on their first call
// op. `InlineManagedPrimitives.expandChainFrom` walks the same halves for the same reason. A terminator
// is never a `param`, but it IS counted and it CAN be an `errorReturn`, so the half this walk would
// otherwise omit is exactly where a throwing body is caught.
//
// ⭐ **THE SHAPE RULES ARE ASKED IN THE SAME WALK AS THE LEAF RULE, so a body is read once**; the shape
// helpers below are shared with `scanSpliceableShape`, which asks them of a called-once candidate and
// nothing else. A leaf scan RETURNS at the first call — the commonest op there is — where the shape scan
// has to read the whole body for its refusal reason.
function scanLeafBody(module StdModule, func IrFunction, framesAreRecorded bool, meta StdOpMetaMemo) returns LeafBodyScan
	match functionShape(func) 'shape'
		spliceable then break 'shape'
		refused then return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: 0)
	end 'shape'

	var ops = 0 as OpCount

	for blockIndex in 0 upto func.blockRefs.count() 'eachBlock'
		let block = module.blockAtPosition(func, position: blockIndex)

		match blockShape(block, blockIndex: blockIndex) 'blockShape'
			spliceable then break 'blockShape'
			refused then return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: ops)
		end 'blockShape'

		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			// Bound ONCE: this runs on every op of every function in the module, and the role is a `match`
			// over a union with ninety variants.
			let role = inlineOpRole(module.getOp(opIdx), meta: meta)

			// A framed op is copyable only where the target records the frame it names.
			if role == InlineOpRole.framed and not framesAreRecorded 'frameWouldBeLost'
				return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: ops)
			end 'frameWouldBeLost'

			match role 'role'
				// A parameter is the call's argument, not an instruction — it is neither counted nor copied.
				parameter or
					body or
					framed then break 'role'

				// ⭐ **RETURNED FROM, NOT FLAGGED — a call is the commonest op there is, and this scan runs
				// over the whole module.** Bailing at the first one is what keeps a 5,000-op function from
				// being walked to the end to learn something its second op already said.
				calling or
					refusing then return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: ops)
			end 'role'

			ops = ops + countedBodyOps(role)
		end 'eachOp'

		if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'terminated'
			ops = ops + 1

			// A `tailCall`-shaped terminator refuses the leaf rule as any call does.
			match terminatorRole(module.getOp(block.terminatorIndex()), func: func, meta: meta) 'termRole'
				body then break 'termRole'
				calling or
					refusing then return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.notALeaf, opCount: ops)
			end 'termRole'
		end 'terminated'
	end 'eachBlock'

	if ops > MaxInlinedLeafOps 'tooBig'
		return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.tooManyOps, opCount: ops)
	end 'tooBig'

	return LeafBodyScan.create(LeafVerdict.eligible, opCount: ops)
end 'scanLeafBody'

// What one body op costs against the budget: everything but a `param`, which becomes the call site's
// argument rather than an instruction. A panic op still counts — it is real code in the callee, and the
// budget is about how much of that this pass is willing to duplicate.
function countedBodyOps(role InlineOpRole) returns OpCount
	return 0 if role == InlineOpRole.parameter else 1
end 'countedBodyOps'

// Whether a body has the SHAPE the splice can copy at all, whichever rule admits it — or which refusal
// names why not.
union SpliceableShape
	spliceable
	refused(reason CalledOnceRefusal)
end 'SpliceableShape'

// The shape both rules ask of a body before their own criterion — the function-level facts, then one
// walk of the blocks. The called-once rule asks it of every candidate; the leaf rule asks the same
// helpers inside its own walk (`scanLeafBody`).
function scanSpliceableShape(module StdModule, func IrFunction, meta StdOpMetaMemo) returns SpliceableShape
	match functionShape(func) 'shape'
		spliceable then break 'shape'
		refused(reason) then return SpliceableShape.refused(reason)
	end 'shape'

	for blockIndex in 0 upto func.blockRefs.count() 'eachBlock'
		let block = module.blockAtPosition(func, position: blockIndex)

		match blockShape(block, blockIndex: blockIndex) 'blockShape'
			spliceable then break 'blockShape'
			refused(reason) then return SpliceableShape.refused(reason)
		end 'blockShape'

		for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
			if inlineOpRole(module.getOp(opIdx), meta: meta) == InlineOpRole.refusing 'unsupported'
				return SpliceableShape.refused(CalledOnceRefusal.unsupportedOp)
			end 'unsupported'
		end 'eachOp'

		if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'terminated'
			let term = module.getOp(block.terminatorIndex())

			match terminatorRole(term, func: func, meta: meta) 'termRole'
				body or
					calling then break 'termRole'
				refusing then return SpliceableShape.refused(terminatorRefusal(term, meta: meta))
			end 'termRole'
		end 'terminated'
	end 'eachBlock'

	return SpliceableShape.spliceable
end 'scanSpliceableShape'

// The function-level shape facts: a by-reference parameter is an ADDRESS whose aliasing the copy cannot
// keep, and a body that carries the green-thread stack guard has a load-bearing FRAME that inlining into
// an exempt caller erases (`IrFunction.needsGreenThreadStackGuard`).
function functionShape(func IrFunction) returns SpliceableShape
	if func.blockRefs.count() == 0 'noBody'
		return SpliceableShape.refused(CalledOnceRefusal.unloweredBody)
	end 'noBody'

	if func.reassignedParamMask != 0 'byReference'
		return SpliceableShape.refused(CalledOnceRefusal.byReferenceParameter)
	end 'byReference'

	if func.needsGreenThreadStackGuard 'guardedFrame'
		return SpliceableShape.refused(CalledOnceRefusal.greenThreadStackGuard)
	end 'guardedFrame'

	return SpliceableShape.spliceable
end 'functionShape'

// The block-level shape facts, asked of the block at `blockIndex` of `func.blockRefs`.
function blockShape(block IrBlock, blockIndex BlockRefIndex) returns SpliceableShape
	// The ENTRY block's phis would have to be fed by the caller's branch into the copy, and there is
	// nothing to feed them with — a function's entry takes its inputs as `param` ops. The parser mints
	// no entry phi, so this is a stated invariant rather than a case; a body that broke it would
	// otherwise be spliced with its entry args unbound.
	if blockIndex == 0 and block.blockArgs.count() != 0 'entryCarriesPhis'
		return SpliceableShape.refused(CalledOnceRefusal.entryCarriesPhis)
	end 'entryCarriesPhis'

	// ⛔⛔ **A BLOCK STILL IN `Terminator.unset` MEANS THE BODY WAS NEVER LOWERED, AND THERE IS
	// NOTHING TO COPY.** `LowerMaxonToStd` RELOCATES every function's blocks and then SKIPS the
	// bodies of the stdlib functions no path from `main` reaches — the function is still pushed
	// (`FunctionNameIndex` counts it for E4015) but not one of its ops, not even its `param` ops, is
	// emitted. What is left looks exactly like a zero-op leaf: no call, no store, no panic, inside
	// any budget — `String.byteLength` would reach `carryOplessExit` with an `unset` entry block, and
	// `Array.clone` would reach `buildValueColumn` with a 2-parameter signature over a 1-wide value
	// space.
	//
	// The test is `isTerminated`, not `hasTerminatorOp`: a `dead` block is legitimately op-less and
	// IS copied (see `carryOplessExit`), and `unset` is the one state that says "unwritten".
	if not block.isTerminated() 'bodyWasNeverLowered'
		return SpliceableShape.refused(CalledOnceRefusal.unloweredBody)
	end 'bodyWasNeverLowered'

	return SpliceableShape.spliceable
end 'blockShape'

// What a terminator slot can hold, for the two scans: a body terminator, a call-shaped one, or one the
// splice cannot copy.
enum TerminatorRole
	body
	calling
	refusing
end 'TerminatorRole'

// The role of the op in a terminator slot. A terminator is never a parameter and never framed; either
// would be a malformed block, and saying so here is cheaper than a rule elsewhere that has to remember it.
function terminatorRole(term StdOp, func IrFunction, meta StdOpMetaMemo) returns TerminatorRole
	match inlineOpRole(term, meta: meta) 'termRole'
		parameter then panic("terminatorRole: '{String.from(func.name)}' has a `param` op in a TERMINATOR slot")
		framed then panic("terminatorRole: '{String.from(func.name)}' has a panic or a divide in a TERMINATOR slot")
		body then return TerminatorRole.body
		calling then return TerminatorRole.calling
		refusing then return TerminatorRole.refusing
	end 'termRole'
end 'terminatorRole'

// Why a refusing terminator refuses: an `errorReturn` — a THROWING body's exit — is caught exactly here.
function terminatorRefusal(term StdOp, meta StdOpMetaMemo) returns CalledOnceRefusal
	return CalledOnceRefusal.throwing if meta.hasRole(term, role: OpRole.errorReturn) else CalledOnceRefusal.unsupportedOp
end 'terminatorRefusal'

// ⛔⛔ **THE SAFE-POINT BYTE DESCRIBES A WHOLE SYMBOL, SO EVERY BYTE INSIDE ONE MUST AGREE ON WHETHER A
// THREAD MAY BE STOPPED THERE.** `__symtable` carries ONE flag per SYMBOL
// (`BacktraceFormat.SymtabSafeFromBias`) and each backend derives it from the function's OWN provenance
// (`TargetPrinter.skipsGreenThreadStackGuard`, read by both `emitFunctionChunk`s). A splice moves code
// ACROSS that boundary, and a body the compiler owns — the scheduler core, the memory manager, the shims —
// spliced into a body it does not would sit inside a symbol the safe-point predicate answers PREEMPTIBLE
// for, with no per-byte channel left to say otherwise. A thread stopped there is stopped in code that must
// never be stopped.
//
// ⚠ **ONE DIRECTION ONLY.** The reverse — an ordinary body spliced into the scaffolding — only takes stop
// opportunities AWAY, which costs a little latency and never correctness, so it stays admitted.
//
// ⚠ It cannot be asked in `scanSpliceableShape`, which sees a callee and no caller: this is a fact about
// a PAIR. Each rule asks it where its OWN caller is known, and the two places are not the same one:
//   * the CALLED-ONCE rule at the census, which is the only gate it has — a callee refused there never
//     becomes a candidate, is never admitted to a wave, and `spliceCalledOnceSites` splices from the
//     positions the census recorded rather than by walking the caller's blocks, so there is no later
//     per-site door to put this behind.
//   * the LEAF rule per SITE (`SiteSplicer.considerSite`), because one eligible leaf is spliced at every
//     site it has and its callers need not agree about provenance.
function splicingWouldWidenTheSafePoint(callee IrFunction, caller IrFunction) returns bool
	return skipsGreenThreadStackGuard(callee) and not skipsGreenThreadStackGuard(caller)
end 'splicingWouldWidenTheSafePoint'

// The site census, accumulated across every caller — the pass's report. Site counts, not callee counts:
// the plan already carries the callee tallies, and a callee refused at 16 ops that nothing calls costs
// nothing.
type InlineLeafStats
	export var inlinedSites as SiteCount
	export var sitesRefusedBySize as SiteCount
	export var sitesRefusedBySizeWithinLargerBudget as SiteCount
	export var selfCallSites as SiteCount

	// Sites refused because the callee is the compiler's own scaffolding and this caller is not — see
	// `splicingWouldWidenTheSafePoint`.
	export var sitesRefusedBySafePoint as SiteCount

	// EC7 — how many spliced parameter cascades this compile proved dead and did not copy. Reported for
	// `ReportedLargerLeafBudget`'s reason: it is the number the row is graded on, and it should come from the
	// compile being asked about rather than be re-derived from a corpus later.
	export var declinedParamGuards as SiteCount

	export static function create() returns InlineLeafStats
		return Self{inlinedSites: 0, sitesRefusedBySize: 0, sitesRefusedBySizeWithinLargerBudget: 0, selfCallSites: 0, sitesRefusedBySafePoint: 0, declinedParamGuards: 0}
	end 'create'

	export function noteDeclinedParamGuard()
		self.declinedParamGuards = self.declinedParamGuards + 1
	end 'noteDeclinedParamGuard'

	export function noteInlined()
		self.inlinedSites = self.inlinedSites + 1
	end 'noteInlined'

	export function noteSelfCall()
		self.selfCallSites = self.selfCallSites + 1
	end 'noteSelfCall'

	export function noteSafePointRefusal()
		self.sitesRefusedBySafePoint = self.sitesRefusedBySafePoint + 1
	end 'noteSafePointRefusal'

	export function noteSizeRefusal(withinLargerBudget bool)
		self.sitesRefusedBySize = self.sitesRefusedBySize + 1

		if withinLargerBudget 'wouldFitLarger'
			self.sitesRefusedBySizeWithinLargerBudget = self.sitesRefusedBySizeWithinLargerBudget + 1
		end 'wouldFitLarger'
	end 'noteSizeRefusal'
end 'InlineLeafStats'

// ⭐⭐ **WHICH OF THE CALLEE'S PARAMETER CASCADES *THIS* CALL SITE PROVES DEAD (EC7) — AND THE TWO SHAPES
// THE SPLICE NEEDS THAT ANSWER IN.**
//
// A narrowed parameter's entry cascade stands in front of EVERY caller, which is what makes it emittable at
// all (`InsertRangeChecks.guardRangedParams`). Inlined, it is no longer in front of every caller — it is in
// front of ONE, and that one may already know the argument is in range. Declining it here is the only place
// that fact can be spent: the callee cannot see it and the caller cannot reach into the callee.
//
// ⚠ **THE BLOCKS ARE `InsertRangeChecks`' ENUMERATION, NOT A WALK OF OUR OWN** — the pass that minted a
// cascade is the one that knows which blocks are its own; a CFG walk from the head would have to tell the
// cascade's blocks from the body's by shape.
type DeclinedGuards
	// Callee `BlockId` -> is this block part of a cascade this site declined? Such a block is copied by
	// NEITHER walk, exactly as a panic block is not — and this is asked FIRST, ahead of the panic test,
	// because a declined cascade's `__rc_panic` is one of its blocks.
	var declined as BoolArray

	// Callee `BlockId` -> where control goes instead when the cascade beginning at this block is declined;
	// every other id maps to itself. Written only for a declined cascade's HEAD.
	var forward as BlockIdArray

	// The callee's block-id space — held rather than spent at construction, because the columns are seeded
	// only if something is actually declined.
	var blockIdSpace as BlockId

	// The number of cascades, which BOUNDS the forwarding chase below: cascades chain (the k-th guard
	// splits the (k-1)-th's `__rc_ok`), so a declined guard's continuation can itself be a declined head.
	var guardCount as GuardCount

	// ⭐ Has anything been declined? It is ALSO the seeded bit for the two columns, and they are one fact:
	// the columns are written in exactly one place and it is the first decline.
	export var anyDeclined as bool

	// ⚠ `export` for ONE reader that is not a method: `sharedDeclinesNothing`, a module-level `let`, which
	// a file-private constructor may not initialize.
	export static function create(blockIdSpace BlockId, guardCount GuardCount) returns DeclinedGuards
		return Self{declined: sharedEmptyDeclineColumn, forward: sharedEmptyForwardColumn, blockIdSpace: blockIdSpace, guardCount: guardCount, anyDeclined: false}
	end 'create'

	function decline(guard RangeCheckParamGuard)
		if not self.anyDeclined 'stillShared'
			self.seedColumns()
			self.anyDeclined = true
		end 'stillShared'

		try self.forward.set(guard.headBlockId, value: guard.okBlockId) otherwise panic("DeclinedGuards.decline: cascade head {guard.headBlockId} is outside the callee's block-id space")

		for blockId in guard.ownedBlockIds 'eachOwnedBlock'
			try self.declined.set(blockId, value: true) otherwise panic("DeclinedGuards.decline: cascade block {blockId} is outside the callee's block-id space")
		end 'eachOwnedBlock'
	end 'decline'

	// COPY-ON-WRITE off the two shared empties, at the first decline and nowhere else. `forward` is seeded
	// to the IDENTITY so that "this block is not forwarded" needs no second column to say so.
	function seedColumns()
		var declineColumn = BoolArray.create()
		declineColumn.resize(self.blockIdSpace)
		self.declined = declineColumn
		var forwardColumn = BlockIdArray.create()
		forwardColumn.resize(self.blockIdSpace)

		for id in 0 upto self.blockIdSpace 'eachBlockId'
			try forwardColumn.set(id, value: id) otherwise panic("DeclinedGuards.seedColumns: forwardColumn.set OOB at {id} inside the space it was just resized to")
		end 'eachBlockId'

		self.forward = forwardColumn
	end 'seedColumns'

	export function isDeclined(calleeBlockId BlockId) returns bool
		if not self.anyDeclined 'noColumnToRead'
			return false
		end 'noColumnToRead'

		return try self.declined.get(calleeBlockId) otherwise panic("DeclinedGuards.isDeclined: callee block id {calleeBlockId} is outside the {self.declined.count()}-block id space this plan was built over")
	end 'isDeclined'

	// Where control ends up entering, following as many declined cascades as chain off `id`. The chase is
	// BOUNDED by the cascade count and panics rather than spinning: `forward` is written only at heads and
	// only ever forward along one chain, so a cycle would mean the record is not the chain it claims to be —
	// and the cost of believing it would be a splice branching into a block it never copied.
	export function entryFrom(id BlockId) returns BlockId
		if not self.anyDeclined 'noColumnToRead'
			return id
		end 'noColumnToRead'

		var target = id

		for _ in 0 upto self.guardCount + 1 'chase'
			let next = try self.forward.get(target) otherwise panic("DeclinedGuards.entryFrom: block id {target} is outside the {self.forward.count()}-block id space this plan was built over")

			if next == target 'settled'
				return target
			end 'settled'

			target = next
		end 'chase'

		panic("DeclinedGuards.entryFrom: the forwarding chain from block {id} is longer than the {self.guardCount} cascade(s) that can be on it — a cascade's `__rc_ok` has been recorded as its own head")
	end 'entryFrom'
end 'DeclinedGuards'

// ⭐ THE ONE PLAN EVERY SPLICE INTO A CALLEE WITH NO NARROWED PARAMETER ANSWERS FROM — which is the
// overwhelming majority of them. `sharedUnseededLeafMinter`'s pattern and its invariant: `decline` is the
// only thing that writes to a plan, and `declinedGuards` hands this one back exactly when the loop that
// would call it has nothing to iterate. Its `blockIdSpace`/`guardCount` are 0 and unread, because both
// columns and the chase are gated on `anyDeclined`, which is false here for ever.
let sharedDeclinesNothing = DeclinedGuards.create(0, guardCount: 0)

// ⭐ The columns ONE splice derives from its callee's block set, held together because every step of the
// splice reads at least two of them.
type SpliceBlocks
	// Callee `BlockId` → the caller `BlockId` a target resolves to: the copied block's fresh id.
	export var blockSubst as BlockIdArray

	// The fresh blocks, in callee block order, skipping the declined cascades — so the copy walk can pair
	// them up positionally with the same skip rather than by a second id lookup.
	export var copiedRefs as BlockRefArray

	// EC7 — the cascades this site declined. It rides here so the MINT walk and the COPY walk skip the same
	// blocks off the same column rather than each re-deciding it; that pairing is positional
	// (`copiedRefs`), and two answers to "is this block copied?" would silently pair a body with the wrong
	// fresh block.
	export var guards as DeclinedGuards

	// This splice's site in the caller, and callee site index → the caller site each of the callee's own
	// splices was re-recorded under (`mapCalleeSites`). A copied block carries the second where it names a
	// callee site and the first where it names none.
	export var spliceSite as InlineSiteRef
	export var siteSubst as InlineSiteRefArray

	static function create(blockIdSpace BlockId, guards DeclinedGuards, spliceSite InlineSiteRef) returns SpliceBlocks
		var blockSubst = BlockIdArray.create()
		blockSubst.resize(blockIdSpace)
		return Self{blockSubst: blockSubst, copiedRefs: BlockRefArray.create(), guards: guards, spliceSite: spliceSite, siteSubst: InlineSiteRefArray.create()}
	end 'create'

	export function targetOf(calleeBlockId BlockId) returns BlockId
		return try self.blockSubst.get(calleeBlockId) otherwise panic("SpliceBlocks.targetOf: callee block id {calleeBlockId} is outside the {self.blockSubst.count()}-block id space this splice mapped")
	end 'targetOf'

	// Re-record every site of the callee in the caller, nested under this splice's site where the callee
	// spliced into its own text. In index order, which is parent-before-child order by construction, so a
	// nested site's parent is already mapped when it is reached.
	function mapCalleeSites(calleeFunc IrFunction, caller IrFunction)
		for site in calleeFunc.inlineSites 'eachSite'
			let parent = match site.parent 'p'
				none gives self.spliceSite
				site(index) gives self.mappedSite(index)
			end 'p'

			self.siteSubst.push(caller.recordInlineSite(site.calleeName, parent: parent))
		end 'eachSite'
	end 'mapCalleeSites'

	function mappedSite(calleeSiteIndex InlineSiteIndex) returns InlineSiteRef
		return try self.siteSubst.get(calleeSiteIndex) otherwise panic("SpliceBlocks.mappedSite: callee site {calleeSiteIndex} is not among the {self.siteSubst.count()} mapped so far — a site nests under one recorded before it")
	end 'mappedSite'

	// The site a copy of a callee block carries in the caller.
	export function siteOf(calleeBlockSite InlineSiteRef) returns InlineSiteRef
		return match calleeBlockSite 's'
			none gives self.spliceSite
			site(index) gives self.mappedSite(index)
		end 's'
	end 'siteOf'
end 'SpliceBlocks'

// Expand every called-once call site, then every inlinable tiny-leaf call site, in the module.
export function inlineLeaves(project Project, module StdModule)
	// ⚠ **THE NON-REFUSING NAME INDEX, for `PromoteStackRecords`' reason exactly.** A duplicate function
	// name is E4015's to report, and the site that owns that refusal runs LATER
	// (`DeadFunctionElimination` is where a runtime-symbol collision becomes visible). Raising it here
	// would move the refusal earlier and change which diagnostic a program gets, so this declines to
	// reason instead: with two bodies of one name, an inline could splice the wrong one.
	match FunctionNameIndex.build(module.functions) 'names'
		duplicateAt then return
		built(byName) then inlineWithNameIndex(project, module: module, byName: byName)
	end 'names'
end 'inlineLeaves'

function inlineWithNameIndex(project Project, module StdModule, byName FunctionNameIndex)
	var def = StdDefinedValues.create()
	var cloner = StdOpCloner.create()
	var meta = StdOpMetaMemo.create()
	let namedByGolden = goldenRequestedFunctions(project, module: module, byName: byName)
	spliceCalledOnceCallees(project, module: module, byName: byName, def: def, cloner: cloner, meta: meta, namedByGolden: namedByGolden)
	spliceLeaves(project, module: module, byName: byName, def: def, cloner: cloner, meta: meta, namedByGolden: namedByGolden)
end 'inlineWithNameIndex'

// ⭐ **A BODY A GOLDEN REQUEST NAMES IS A REFERENCE NEITHER RULE MAY SPLICE AWAY.** `--emit-ir-runtime=`
// (a ```RequiredRuntime block) asks the printer for that body after the pipeline; a callee whose every
// site was spliced is dropped by dead-function elimination, and the printer would then have nothing to
// render. Function index -> named by the request.
function goldenRequestedFunctions(project Project, module StdModule, byName FunctionNameIndex) returns BoolArray
	var named = BoolArray.create()
	named.resize(module.funcCount())

	for name in project.requestedRuntimeBodies 'eachRequested'
		let funcIndex = try byName.byName.get(name.toByteArray()) otherwise continue
		try named.set(funcIndex, value: true) otherwise panic("goldenRequestedFunctions: function index {funcIndex} is outside the {named.count()}-function module")
	end 'eachRequested'

	return named
end 'goldenRequestedFunctions'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// The leaf rule
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

function spliceLeaves(project Project, module StdModule, byName FunctionNameIndex, def StdDefinedValues, cloner StdOpCloner, meta StdOpMetaMemo, namedByGolden BoolArray)
	let plan = buildLeafPlan(module, framesAreRecorded: targetEmitsInlineFrameRecords(project.target), namedByGolden: namedByGolden, meta: meta)

	if plan.eligibleCount == 0 'nothingToInline'
		logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "inlineLeaves: 0 site(s) inlined, 0 callee(s) eligible")
		return
	end 'nothingToInline'

	// Built ONCE, over the eligible callees alone — see `ValueOriginTable.indexRowsOfFunctions` for why
	// the alternative (`lookup` per copied value) is a quadratic against a table with one row per
	// value-producing op in the program.
	let origins = project.valueOrigins.indexRowsOfFunctions(plan.eligibleFuncs, funcCount: module.funcCount() as FuncIndex)
	var stats = InlineLeafStats.create()
	let admission = SpliceAdmission.leaves(plan)

	for funcIndex in 0 upto module.funcCount() 'eachFunc'
		let func = module.getFunc(funcIndex)

		// ⭐ A STDLIB FUNCTION NO PATH FROM `main` REACHES IS SKIPPED — the same reachability fact
		// `insertRangeChecks` and `inlineManagedPrimitives` skip on. Here it is purely cost:
		// `eliminateDeadFunctions` drops the function before isel either way, and this pass registers no
		// `.rdata`, so skipping cannot move a golden.
		if project.stdlibFacts.unreachable.contains(func.name) 'notInTheProgram'
			continue
		end 'notInTheProgram'

		var splicer = SiteSplicer.create(project, module: module, func: func, funcIndex: funcIndex, byName: byName, admission: admission, origins: origins, stats: stats, def: def, cloner: cloner, meta: meta, ids: sharedUnseededLeafMinter, seeded: false)
		splicer.run()
	end 'eachFunc'

	let inlined = stats.inlinedSites
	let eligible = plan.eligibleCount
	let sizeRefusals = stats.sitesRefusedBySize
	let sizeRefusalsAtLargerBudget = stats.sitesRefusedBySizeWithinLargerBudget
	let selfCalls = stats.selfCallSites
	let safePointRefusals = stats.sitesRefusedBySafePoint
	let declinedGuards = stats.declinedParamGuards
	let calleesSizeRefused = plan.tooManyOpsCount
	let calleesWithinLargerBudget = plan.tooManyOpsWithinLargerBudget
	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "inlineLeaves: {inlined} site(s) inlined, {eligible} callee(s) eligible, {sizeRefusals} refused-by-size at {MaxInlinedLeafOps} ops ({calleesSizeRefused} callee(s)) of which {sizeRefusalsAtLargerBudget} site(s) / {calleesWithinLargerBudget} callee(s) a budget of {ReportedLargerLeafBudget} would admit, {selfCalls} self-call site(s), {safePointRefusals} site(s) refused as the compiler's own scaffolding spliced into code that is not, {declinedGuards} spliced parameter cascade(s) declined as provably dead")
end 'spliceLeaves'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// The called-once rule
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Why a called-once callee was left as a call. Counted per reason into the pass summary and named in the
// per-callee trace.
enum CalledOnceRefusal
	// Its one `call` site is not its only reference: a `tryCall` site, a function-value thunk, a witness or
	// layout slot, or a name the entry stub or a hand-assembled chunk calls.
	referencedElsewhere

	// Its one site is inside itself, or it lies on a cycle of called-once callees: splicing any member
	// would nest a body into itself.
	recursive
	throwing
	byReferenceParameter
	greenThreadStackGuard

	// The callee is the compiler's own scaffolding and the caller is not, so one symbol would carry both
	// answers to "may a thread be stopped here" — see `splicingWouldWidenTheSafePoint`.
	safePointProvenance
	unloweredBody
	entryCarriesPhis
	unsupportedOp
	pressureBudgetExceeded

	// The target emits no inline frame records, so a trace raised inside any spliced body would lose the
	// callee's frame with nothing to restore it — every called-once callee stays a call there.
	noInlineFrameRecords

	// A ```RequiredRuntime golden asks the printer for this body after the pipeline; spliced away, it
	// would be dropped and the golden would pin nothing.
	namedByGoldenRequest
end 'CalledOnceRefusal'

function calledOnceRefusalText(reason CalledOnceRefusal) returns String
	return match reason 'r'
		referencedElsewhere gives "it is reached by more than its one call"
		recursive gives "its one site is inside itself or a cycle of called-once callees"
		throwing gives "it throws"
		byReferenceParameter gives "it takes a by-reference parameter"
		greenThreadStackGuard gives "it carries the green-thread stack guard"
		safePointProvenance gives "it is the compiler's own scaffolding and its caller is not, so the spliced bytes could be stopped where the callee never may be"
		unloweredBody gives "its body was never lowered"
		entryCarriesPhis gives "its entry block carries phis"
		unsupportedOp gives "it holds an op the splice cannot copy"
		pressureBudgetExceeded gives "the caller's values live across the site plus the body's loop live-ins are over the register budget"
		noInlineFrameRecords gives "the target emits no inline frame records to keep its frame in a trace"
		namedByGoldenRequest gives "a golden request names its body"
	end 'r'
end 'calledOnceRefusalText'

// The one-walk site census over every function's live ops: how many direct `call` ops name each
// function, which function holds the one site of a function called once and WHERE in it, and which
// functions something other than a direct call reaches.
//
// ⭐ **THE SITE'S COORDINATES ARE TAKEN HERE, where the walk already stands on the op** — the block
// position and the op's position in it — so a caller's sites are never searched for again. They stay
// true until the caller is first spliced into: nothing but a splice moves an op of a live function, and a
// caller's facts (`CallerSiteFacts`) are taken before its first.
type CalledOnceCensus
	var siteCounts as SiteCountArray
	var callerOf as FuncIndexArray
	var sitePositions as BlockPositionColumn
	var siteOpPositions as BlockOpPosArray
	var referencedElsewhere as BoolArray

	// The enumerator's sink, refilled per op of the walk.
	var reference as StdFunctionReference

	static function take(project Project, module StdModule, byName FunctionNameIndex) returns CalledOnceCensus
		let funcCount = module.funcCount() as FuncIndex
		var siteCounts = SiteCountArray.create()
		siteCounts.resize(funcCount)
		var callerOf = FuncIndexArray.create()
		callerOf.resize(funcCount)
		var sitePositions = BlockPositionColumn.create()
		sitePositions.resize(funcCount)
		var siteOpPositions = BlockOpPosArray.create()
		siteOpPositions.resize(funcCount)
		var referencedElsewhere = BoolArray.create()
		referencedElsewhere.resize(funcCount)
		var census = Self{siteCounts: siteCounts, callerOf: callerOf, sitePositions: sitePositions, siteOpPositions: siteOpPositions, referencedElsewhere: referencedElsewhere, reference: StdFunctionReference.create()}
		census.markRoots(project, byName: byName)

		// The blocks are walked directly rather than through `IrModule.liveOpIndices`, which materializes an
		// index array per function of the program to say the same thing.
		for funcIndex in 0 upto funcCount 'eachFunc'
			let func = module.getFunc(funcIndex)

			for position in 0 upto func.blockRefs.count() 'eachBlock'
				let block = module.blockAtPosition(func, position: position)

				for opPos in 0 upto block.opRefs.count() 'eachOp'
					let opIdx = try block.opRefs.get(opPos) otherwise panic("CalledOnceCensus.take: opRefs.get OOB at {opPos} inside its own count")
					census.noteReferences(module.getOp(opIdx), from: funcIndex, position: position, opPos: opPos, byName: byName)
				end 'eachOp'

				if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'terminated'
					census.noteTerminatorReferences(module.getOp(block.terminatorIndex()), func: func, byName: byName)
				end 'terminated'
			end 'eachBlock'
		end 'eachFunc'

		return census
	end 'take'

	// A function called by exactly one direct `call` op and by nothing else.
	function isCalledOnce(funcIndex FuncIndex) returns bool
		return self.siteCountOf(funcIndex) == 1 and not self.isReferencedElsewhere(funcIndex)
	end 'isCalledOnce'

	function siteCountOf(funcIndex FuncIndex) returns SiteCount
		return try self.siteCounts.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("CalledOnceCensus.siteCountOf: function index {funcIndex} is outside the {self.siteCounts.count()}-function module")
	end 'siteCountOf'

	function isReferencedElsewhere(funcIndex FuncIndex) returns bool
		return try self.referencedElsewhere.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("CalledOnceCensus.isReferencedElsewhere: function index {funcIndex} is outside the {self.referencedElsewhere.count()}-function module")
	end 'isReferencedElsewhere'

	// The function holding a called-once callee's one site.
	function callerOfCallee(calleeIndex FuncIndex) returns FuncIndex
		self.requireCalledOnce(calleeIndex)
		return try self.callerOf.get(calleeIndex) otherwise panic("CalledOnceCensus.callerOfCallee: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.callerOf.count()}-function module")
	end 'callerOfCallee'

	// Where in its caller a called-once callee's one site stands: the block's position in the caller's
	// `blockRefs`, and the call's position in that block.
	function sitePositionOf(calleeIndex FuncIndex) returns BlockRefIndex
		self.requireCalledOnce(calleeIndex)
		return try self.sitePositions.get(calleeIndex) otherwise panic("CalledOnceCensus.sitePositionOf: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.sitePositions.count()}-function module")
	end 'sitePositionOf'

	function siteOpPositionOf(calleeIndex FuncIndex) returns BlockOpPos
		self.requireCalledOnce(calleeIndex)
		return try self.siteOpPositions.get(calleeIndex) otherwise panic("CalledOnceCensus.siteOpPositionOf: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.siteOpPositions.count()}-function module")
	end 'siteOpPositionOf'

	function requireCalledOnce(calleeIndex FuncIndex)
		if not self.isCalledOnce(calleeIndex) 'notCalledOnce'
			panic("CalledOnceCensus: function {calleeIndex} has {self.siteCountOf(calleeIndex)} site(s) — the caller and the site are facts about a function called exactly once")
		end 'notCalledOnce'
	end 'requireCalledOnce'

	// ⭐ **THE REFERENCES NO `call` OP SPELLS — `DeadFunctionElimination.seedRoots`' three kinds, asked here
	// as "reached by more than its call".** The entry and every test body are called from code built after
	// this pass; the compiler-owned names are called from the entry stub or a hand-assembled chunk; a
	// function an `.rdata` slot names is reached through data. Splicing any of them would copy a body that
	// stays alive anyway.
	function markRoots(project Project, byName FunctionNameIndex)
		self.markReferenced(project.entryFunctionName, byName: byName)

		for name in compilerOwnedRootNames(project.target) 'eachOwnedRoot'
			self.markReferenced(name, byName: byName)
		end 'eachOwnedRoot'

		for func in byName.functions 'eachFunction'
			if isTestFunctionName(func.name) 'aTest'
				self.markReferenced(func.name, byName: byName)
			end 'aTest'
		end 'eachFunction'

		for reloc in project.globalData.pendingRdataRelocs 'eachRdataSlot'
			self.markReferenced(reloc.funcName, byName: byName)
		end 'eachRdataSlot'

		// A function used as a VALUE is called by its `__fnref_` thunk, which does not exist until after the
		// pipeline (`installFnRefThunks`): the call this module cannot show yet is counted from the target list
		// the thunk builder reads.
		for name in project.functionValueTargets 'eachValueTarget'
			self.markReferenced(name, byName: byName)
		end 'eachValueTarget'
	end 'markRoots'

	// A name the module does not define is a hand-assembled chunk or an uninstalled runtime: nothing to mark.
	function markReferenced(name ByteArray, byName FunctionNameIndex)
		let funcIndex = try byName.byName.get(name) otherwise return
		self.markReferencedIndex(funcIndex)
	end 'markReferenced'

	function markReferencedIndex(funcIndex FuncIndex)
		try self.referencedElsewhere.set(funcIndex, value: true) otherwise panic("CalledOnceCensus.markReferenced: function index {funcIndex} is outside the {self.referencedElsewhere.count()}-function module")
	end 'markReferencedIndex'

	function noteCall(calleeIndex FuncIndex, from FuncIndex, position BlockRefIndex, opPos BlockOpPos)
		try self.siteCounts.set(calleeIndex, value: self.siteCountOf(calleeIndex) + 1) otherwise panic("CalledOnceCensus.noteCall: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.siteCounts.count()}-function module")
		try self.callerOf.set(calleeIndex, value: from) otherwise panic("CalledOnceCensus.noteCall: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.callerOf.count()}-function module")
		try self.sitePositions.set(calleeIndex, value: position) otherwise panic("CalledOnceCensus.noteCall: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.sitePositions.count()}-function module")
		try self.siteOpPositions.set(calleeIndex, value: opPos) otherwise panic("CalledOnceCensus.noteCall: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.siteOpPositions.count()}-function module")
	end 'noteCall'

	// The references are `DeadFunctionElimination`'s own enumeration (`stdFunctionReferenceOf`), so the
	// set this census counts and the set that walk keeps alive cannot drift apart: a callee spliced and
	// emptied here must be one nothing but its call reaches there.
	function noteReferences(op StdOp, from FuncIndex, position BlockRefIndex, opPos BlockOpPos, byName FunctionNameIndex)
		stdFunctionReferenceOf(op, byName: byName.byName, into: self.reference)

		match self.reference.kind 'reference'
			none then break 'reference'
			directCall then self.noteCall(self.reference.funcIndex, from: from, position: position, opPos: opPos)
			otherReference then self.markReferencedIndex(self.reference.funcIndex)
		end 'reference'
	end 'noteReferences'

	// A terminator slot never holds a direct call — every call the Std lowering emits is a body op — so
	// a site there would be one the splice could not find, and it is refused rather than counted.
	function noteTerminatorReferences(op StdOp, func IrFunction, byName FunctionNameIndex)
		stdFunctionReferenceOf(op, byName: byName.byName, into: self.reference)

		match self.reference.kind 'reference'
			none then break 'reference'
			directCall then panic("CalledOnceCensus: '{String.from(func.name)}' holds a direct call of function {self.reference.funcIndex} in a TERMINATOR slot — every call is a body op, and the splice cuts a block immediately past the call it replaces")
			otherReference then self.markReferencedIndex(self.reference.funcIndex)
		end 'reference'
	end 'noteTerminatorReferences'
end 'CalledOnceCensus'

// The called-once callees one wave admits — those whose every called-once callee is already spliced — and
// the value-space width and block count each copy was admitted at.
type CalledOnceWave
	var admitted as BoolArray
	var valueBounds as ValueIdArray
	var blockCounts as BlockPositionColumn

	static function create(funcCount FuncIndex) returns CalledOnceWave
		var admitted = BoolArray.create()
		admitted.resize(funcCount)
		var valueBounds = ValueIdArray.create()
		valueBounds.resize(funcCount)
		var blockCounts = BlockPositionColumn.create()
		blockCounts.resize(funcCount)
		return Self{admitted: admitted, valueBounds: valueBounds, blockCounts: blockCounts}
	end 'create'

	function admit(calleeIndex FuncIndex, valueBound ValueId, blockCount BlockRefIndex)
		try self.admitted.set(calleeIndex, value: true) otherwise panic("CalledOnceWave.admit: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.admitted.count()}-function module")
		try self.valueBounds.set(calleeIndex, value: valueBound) otherwise panic("CalledOnceWave.admit: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.valueBounds.count()}-function module")
		try self.blockCounts.set(calleeIndex, value: blockCount) otherwise panic("CalledOnceWave.admit: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.blockCounts.count()}-function module")
	end 'admit'

	function admits(calleeIndex FuncIndex) returns bool
		return try self.admitted.get(calleeIndex) otherwise panic("CalledOnceWave.admits: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.admitted.count()}-function module")
	end 'admits'

	// Clear one callee's admission once its wave is done, so the columns serve every wave without being
	// re-sized to the module per wave.
	function forget(calleeIndex FuncIndex)
		try self.admitted.set(calleeIndex, value: false) otherwise panic("CalledOnceWave.forget: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.admitted.count()}-function module")
	end 'forget'

	function valueBoundOf(calleeIndex FuncIndex) returns ValueId
		return try self.valueBounds.get(calleeIndex) otherwise panic("CalledOnceWave.valueBoundOf: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.valueBounds.count()}-function module")
	end 'valueBoundOf'

	function blockCountOf(calleeIndex FuncIndex) returns BlockRefIndex
		return try self.blockCounts.get(calleeIndex) otherwise panic("CalledOnceWave.blockCountOf: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the {self.blockCounts.count()}-function module")
	end 'blockCountOf'
end 'CalledOnceWave'

// ⭐ **EVERYTHING THE SPLICES INTO ONE CALLER ASK ABOUT IT, COMPUTED ONCE PER CALLER**: every candidate
// site in it — where it stands, what it passes, and the values live across it that the loop around it
// reads (`spliceFitsRegisters`' caller half) — plus the caller's fresh-id counter. Read at every wave the
// caller is spliced in; a liveness per (caller, wave) cost `main` — a caller in every wave one of its
// callees becomes ready in — the whole program each time.
//
// The sites come from the census's coordinates, taken before anything moved: a candidate's site is in the
// caller's own text (a body copied in holds no candidate, every called-once callee of it having been
// spliced or refused before it was), and a splice into this caller moves only the ops behind the site it
// replaces, which `SiteSplicer.spliceCalledOnceSites` re-coordinates as it goes. So a site's live-across
// list is what it was when the caller was first met — a splice elsewhere in the caller moves no value's
// def or use past another site, defines nothing another site's loop reads that the call it replaced did
// not, and adds no block outside the region the call held.
type CallerSiteFacts
	// Every candidate site of the caller, in the order the census's caller grouping lists the callees. A
	// handful per caller, so a callee's site is found by a scan of them.
	export var sites as SiteAcrossArray

	// The caller's fresh-`ValueId` counter. Seeded past every id the caller uses before its first splice;
	// after each wave `SiteSplicer` re-seeds it to one past the highest id a copy DEFINED, since the copy of
	// a callee's value space mints an id for every index and a trailing gap in that space would otherwise
	// hold the counter above where a fresh seed from the function's ops would put it.
	export var ids as ValueMinter

	static function of(module StdModule, func IrFunction, callerIndex FuncIndex, census CalledOnceCensus, calleesOfCallers CsrGraph, byName FunctionNameIndex, liveness StdLiveness, across StdValueUseSet) returns CallerSiteFacts
		let valueBound = nextFreeValueId(module, func: func)
		liveness.recompute(module, func: func, valueBound: valueBound)
		var loops = FunctionLoops.of(func, succ: liveness.succ, pred: liveness.pred)
		var sites = SiteAcrossArray.create()

		for k in 0 upto calleesOfCallers.degree(callerIndex as CsrIndex) 'eachSite'
			let calleeIndex = calleesOfCallers.neighbour(callerIndex as CsrIndex, k: k) as FuncIndex
			let position = census.sitePositionOf(calleeIndex)
			let opPos = census.siteOpPositionOf(calleeIndex)
			let args = siteArgsOf(module, caller: func, position: position, opPos: opPos, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, byName: byName)
			across.clear()
			liveness.collectLiveAcrossOp(module, func: func, position: position, opPos: opPos, into: across)
			loops.keepReadsAround(module, func: func, position: position, valueBound: valueBound, across: across)
			sites.push(SiteAcross.create(calleeIndex, position: position, opPos: opPos, args: args, across: across))
		end 'eachSite'

		return Self{sites: sites, ids: ValueMinter.resuming(valueBound)}
	end 'of'

	function siteOf(calleeIndex FuncIndex, callee IrFunction) returns SiteAcross
		for site in self.sites 'eachSite'
			if site.calleeIndex == calleeIndex 'theSite'
				return site
			end 'theSite'
		end 'eachSite'

		panic("CallerSiteFacts.siteOf: the caller holds no call of '{String.from(callee.name)}', whose one site the census placed there")
	end 'siteOf'
end 'CallerSiteFacts'

// Caller -> its site facts, filled the first wave the caller is spliced into.
typealias CallerSiteFactsMap = Map with (FuncIndex, CallerSiteFacts)

// One candidate site of a caller: where its call stands, what the call passes, and the values live across
// it that the loop around it reads, in the order the liveness listed them. The coordinates FOLLOW the call
// as splices ahead of it in the block move it into a continuation, so they name the op until it is spliced.
type SiteAcross
	export var calleeIndex as FuncIndex
	export var position as BlockRefIndex
	export var opPos as BlockOpPos
	export var args as ValueIdArray
	export var across as ValueIdArray
	export var spliced as bool

	static function create(calleeIndex FuncIndex, position BlockRefIndex, opPos BlockOpPos, args ValueIdArray, across StdValueUseSet) returns SiteAcross
		var ids = ValueIdArray.create()

		for i in 0 upto across.insertedIdCount() 'eachAcross'
			ids.push(across.insertedIdAt(i))
		end 'eachAcross'

		return Self{calleeIndex: calleeIndex, position: position, opPos: opPos, args: args, across: ids, spliced: false}
	end 'create'
end 'SiteAcross'

typealias SiteAcrossArray = Array with SiteAcross

// The argument list of the call at the census's coordinates for `calleeIndex` — checked to BE a direct
// call of that callee, since a coordinate that drifted would otherwise splice a body over some other op.
function siteArgsOf(module StdModule, caller IrFunction, position BlockRefIndex, opPos BlockOpPos, calleeIndex FuncIndex, byName FunctionNameIndex) returns ValueIdArray
	let block = module.blockAtPosition(caller, position: position)
	let opIdx = try block.opRefs.get(opPos) otherwise panic("siteArgsOf: block {position} of '{String.from(caller.name)}' has no op at position {opPos}, where the census placed a call")

	match module.getOp(opIdx) 'kind'
		call(_, callee, args) then return argsOfCensusedCall(callee, args: args, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, caller: caller, byName: byName)
		default panic("siteArgsOf: the op at position {opPos} of block {position} of '{String.from(caller.name)}' is not a direct call, where the census placed one")
	end 'kind'
end 'siteArgsOf'

function argsOfCensusedCall(callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, calleeIndex FuncIndex, caller IrFunction, byName FunctionNameIndex) returns ValueIdArray
	requireCallNamesCallee(callee, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, caller: caller, byName: byName)
	return args
end 'argsOfCensusedCall'

// ⛔ **A SITE'S CALLEE IS A CLAIM, CHECKED WHERE IT IS SPENT.** The called-once rule reaches a site by
// coordinates the census took and every wave's splits move, so the op found there must NAME the callee whose
// body is about to be copied over it — an arity match would let a drifted coordinate splice one function's
// body over a call of another, silently. Asked at the site's first reading and again at the splice.
function requireCallNamesCallee(callee ByteArray, calleeIndex FuncIndex, caller IrFunction, byName FunctionNameIndex)
	let named = try byName.byName.get(callee) otherwise panic("inlineLeaves: a recorded site in '{String.from(caller.name)}' calls '{String.from(callee)}', which the name index does not know")

	if named != calleeIndex 'anotherCallee'
		panic("inlineLeaves: a recorded site in '{String.from(caller.name)}' calls '{String.from(callee)}' (function {named}), not function {calleeIndex} whose site it is recorded as — a site coordinate moved between the census and this read")
	end 'anotherCallee'
end 'requireCallNamesCallee'

// A loop's place in a preorder walk of the nest: a loop's nest is exactly the loops numbered
// `[pre, post]`, so "does this nest hold that loop" is two compares.
typealias NestTour = int(0 to u64.max)
typealias NestTourColumn = Array with NestTour

// The natural loops of one function, in the shape the pressure estimate asks: which loop is the innermost
// around a block, and what that loop reads.
//
// ⭐ **THE NEST IS BUILT WITHOUT COLLECTING EVERY LOOP'S WHOLE MEMBERSHIP.** Headers arrive innermost-first,
// and a walk that reaches a block an inner loop already owns hops to that loop's outermost known ancestor
// and continues from ITS header's predecessors, so each block and edge is walked once per loop that holds
// it DIRECTLY. Collecting each loop in full would cost the nesting depth times the function.
type FunctionLoops
	var blockCount as BlockRefIndex
	var pred as CsrGraph
	var loops as NaturalLoops

	// Block position -> the header of the innermost loop holding it; `blockCount` for a block in no loop.
	var innermostHeaderOf as BlockPositionColumn

	// Header -> the header of the loop directly around it; `blockCount` for an outermost loop.
	var parentHeaderOf as BlockPositionColumn

	// Header -> its preorder number and the last preorder number in its nest, and value id -> the preorder
	// numbers of the loops whose own blocks read it, ascending. Built on the first question.
	var preOf as NestTourColumn
	var postOf as NestTourColumn
	var readTours as CsrGraph
	var toursGathered as bool

	// Over the CFG the caller's liveness already built, in both directions.
	static function of(func IrFunction, succ CsrGraph, pred CsrGraph) returns FunctionLoops
		let blockCount = func.blockRefs.count()
		var loops = Self{blockCount: blockCount, pred: pred, loops: NaturalLoops.find(succ, nodeCount: blockCount as CsrIndex), innermostHeaderOf: filledPositions(blockCount, value: blockCount), parentHeaderOf: filledPositions(blockCount, value: blockCount), preOf: NestTourColumn.create(), postOf: NestTourColumn.create(), readTours: CsrGraph.create(CsrColumn.create(), list: CsrColumn.create()), toursGathered: false}
		loops.assignInnermostHeaders()
		return loops
	end 'of'

	function assignInnermostHeaders()
		var worklist = BlockPositionColumn.create()

		for header in self.loops.headers 'eachLoop'
			let headerPosition = header as BlockRefIndex
			self.own(headerPosition, header: headerPosition)

			for k in 0 upto self.loops.latches.degree(header) 'eachLatch'
				worklist.push(self.loops.latches.neighbour(header, k: k) as BlockRefIndex)
			end 'eachLatch'

			while worklist.count() != 0 'walk'
				let position = try worklist.pop() otherwise panic("FunctionLoops.assignInnermostHeaders: pop from a non-empty worklist")
				self.admit(position, header: headerPosition, worklist: worklist)
			end 'walk'
		end 'eachLoop'
	end 'assignInnermostHeaders'

	// One block reached by the walk from `header`'s latches: a block no loop owns joins this loop and its
	// predecessors follow; a block an inner loop owns nests that loop's outermost known ancestor under this
	// one and continues from that ancestor header's predecessors, unless it is already nested here.
	function admit(position BlockRefIndex, header BlockRefIndex, worklist BlockPositionColumn)
		let owner = self.innermostHeaderAt(position)

		if owner == self.blockCount 'unowned'
			self.own(position, header: header)
			self.pushPredecessors(position, header: header, worklist: worklist)
			return
		end 'unowned'

		let top = self.outermostAncestorOf(owner)

		if top == header 'alreadyInside'
			return
		end 'alreadyInside'

		try self.parentHeaderOf.set(top, value: header) otherwise panic("FunctionLoops.admit: parentHeaderOf.set OOB at {top}")
		self.pushPredecessors(top, header: header, worklist: worklist)
	end 'admit'

	function own(position BlockRefIndex, header BlockRefIndex)
		try self.innermostHeaderOf.set(position, value: header) otherwise panic("FunctionLoops.own: innermostHeaderOf.set OOB at {position}")
	end 'own'

	function pushPredecessors(position BlockRefIndex, header BlockRefIndex, worklist BlockPositionColumn)
		if position == header 'theHeaderItself'
			return
		end 'theHeaderItself'

		for k in 0 upto self.pred.degree(position as CsrIndex) 'eachPredecessor'
			worklist.push(self.pred.neighbour(position as CsrIndex, k: k) as BlockRefIndex)
		end 'eachPredecessor'
	end 'pushPredecessors'

	function outermostAncestorOf(header BlockRefIndex) returns BlockRefIndex
		var top = header

		while self.parentHeaderAt(top) != self.blockCount 'climb'
			top = self.parentHeaderAt(top)
		end 'climb'

		return top
	end 'outermostAncestorOf'

	function innermostHeaderAt(position BlockRefIndex) returns BlockRefIndex
		return try self.innermostHeaderOf.get(position) otherwise panic("FunctionLoops.innermostHeaderAt: block position {position} is outside the {self.blockCount}-block function")
	end 'innermostHeaderAt'

	function parentHeaderAt(header BlockRefIndex) returns BlockRefIndex
		return try self.parentHeaderOf.get(header) otherwise panic("FunctionLoops.parentHeaderAt: block position {header} is outside the {self.blockCount}-block function")
	end 'parentHeaderAt'

	// Narrow `across` to the values the innermost loop around `position` reads, over its whole nest; a
	// site outside every loop keeps them all.
	function keepReadsAround(module StdModule, func IrFunction, position BlockRefIndex, valueBound ValueId, across StdValueUseSet)
		let header = self.innermostHeaderAt(position)

		if header == self.blockCount 'noLoop'
			return
		end 'noLoop'

		self.gatherReadTours(module, func: func, valueBound: valueBound)
		var kept = ValueIdArray.create()

		for i in 0 upto across.insertedIdCount() 'eachAcross'
			let valueId = across.insertedIdAt(i)

			if self.isReadInNest(valueId, header: header) 'read'
				kept.push(valueId)
			end 'read'
		end 'eachAcross'

		across.clear()

		for valueId in kept 'eachKept'
			across.insert(valueId)
		end 'eachKept'
	end 'keepReadsAround'

	// Whether some loop of `header`'s nest reads `valueId`: the value's read tours ascend, so the first
	// one at or past the nest's own number lies inside the nest exactly when it is not past its last.
	function isReadInNest(valueId ValueId, header BlockRefIndex) returns bool
		let pre = self.tourAt(self.preOf, header: header)
		let post = self.tourAt(self.postOf, header: header)
		var lo = 0 as CsrIndex
		var hi = self.readTours.degree(valueId as CsrIndex)

		while lo < hi 'partition'
			let mid = lo + ((hi - lo) shr 1)

			if (self.readTours.neighbour(valueId as CsrIndex, k: mid) as NestTour) < pre 'beforeTheNest'
				lo = mid + 1
			end 'beforeTheNest' else 'atOrPastIt'
				hi = mid
			end 'atOrPastIt'
		end 'partition'

		if lo >= self.readTours.degree(valueId as CsrIndex) 'noReadAtOrPast'
			return false
		end 'noReadAtOrPast'

		return (self.readTours.neighbour(valueId as CsrIndex, k: lo) as NestTour) <= post
	end 'isReadInNest'

	function tourAt(column NestTourColumn, header BlockRefIndex) returns NestTour
		return try column.get(header) otherwise panic("FunctionLoops.tourAt: block position {header} is outside the {self.blockCount}-block function")
	end 'tourAt'

	// The nest numbered by a preorder walk, then every loop's own blocks read in that order, so each
	// value's read tours arrive ascending and the CSR keeps them so. One pass over the function's ops.
	function gatherReadTours(module StdModule, func IrFunction, valueBound ValueId)
		if self.toursGathered 'already'
			return
		end 'already'

		self.toursGathered = true
		let blocksByHeader = self.blocksByHeader()
		let preorder = self.numberNest()
		var reads = StdValueUseSet.createRecordingIds()
		var readValue = CsrColumn.create()
		var readTour = CsrColumn.create()

		for header in preorder 'eachLoopInOrder'
			let tour = self.tourAt(self.preOf, header: header)
			reads.clear()

			for k in 0 upto blocksByHeader.degree(header as CsrIndex) 'eachOwnBlock'
				collectBlockReads(module, block: module.blockAtPosition(func, position: blocksByHeader.neighbour(header as CsrIndex, k: k) as BlockRefIndex), reads: reads)
			end 'eachOwnBlock'

			for i in 0 upto reads.insertedIdCount() 'eachRead'
				readValue.push(reads.insertedIdAt(i) as CsrIndex)
				readTour.push(tour as CsrIndex)
			end 'eachRead'
		end 'eachLoopInOrder'

		self.readTours = buildCsr(readValue, to: readTour, nodeCount: valueBound as CsrIndex)
	end 'gatherReadTours'

	// Header -> the blocks it holds directly.
	function blocksByHeader() returns CsrGraph
		var owner = CsrColumn.create()
		var owned = CsrColumn.create()

		for position in 0 upto self.blockCount 'eachBlock'
			let header = self.innermostHeaderAt(position)

			if header != self.blockCount 'inALoop'
				owner.push(header as CsrIndex)
				owned.push(position as CsrIndex)
			end 'inALoop'
		end 'eachBlock'

		return buildCsr(owner, to: owned, nodeCount: self.blockCount as CsrIndex)
	end 'blocksByHeader'

	// Number every loop in preorder over the nest — roots first, each nest contiguous — filling `preOf`
	// and `postOf`, and answer the headers in that order.
	function numberNest() returns BlockPositionColumn
		var childOf = CsrColumn.create()
		var child = CsrColumn.create()
		var roots = BlockPositionColumn.create()

		for header in self.loops.headers 'eachLoop'
			let parent = self.parentHeaderAt(header as BlockRefIndex)

			if parent == self.blockCount 'outermost'
				roots.push(header as BlockRefIndex)
			end 'outermost' else 'nested'
				childOf.push(parent as CsrIndex)
				child.push(header)
			end 'nested'
		end 'eachLoop'

		let children = buildCsr(childOf, to: child, nodeCount: self.blockCount as CsrIndex)
		self.preOf = filledTours(self.blockCount)
		self.postOf = filledTours(self.blockCount)
		var preorder = BlockPositionColumn.create()
		var stack = BlockPositionColumn.create()
		var nextChild = CsrColumn.create()
		var next = 0 as NestTour

		for root in roots 'eachRoot'
			stack.push(root)
			nextChild.push(0)

			while stack.count() != 0 'walk'
				let top = stack.count() - 1
				let header = try stack.get(top) otherwise panic("FunctionLoops.numberNest: stack.get OOB at {top}")
				let k = try nextChild.get(top) otherwise panic("FunctionLoops.numberNest: nextChild.get OOB at {top}")

				if k == 0 'entering'
					try self.preOf.set(header, value: next) otherwise panic("FunctionLoops.numberNest: preOf.set OOB at {header}")
					preorder.push(header)
					next = next + 1
				end 'entering'

				if k >= children.degree(header as CsrIndex) 'childrenDone'
					try self.postOf.set(header, value: next - 1) otherwise panic("FunctionLoops.numberNest: postOf.set OOB at {header}")
					_ = try stack.pop() otherwise panic("FunctionLoops.numberNest: pop from a non-empty stack")
					_ = try nextChild.pop() otherwise panic("FunctionLoops.numberNest: pop from a non-empty stack")
					continue
				end 'childrenDone'

				try nextChild.set(top, value: k + 1) otherwise panic("FunctionLoops.numberNest: nextChild.set OOB at {top}")
				stack.push(children.neighbour(header as CsrIndex, k: k) as BlockRefIndex)
				nextChild.push(0)
			end 'walk'
		end 'eachRoot'

		return preorder
	end 'numberNest'
end 'FunctionLoops'

function filledPositions(count BlockRefIndex, value BlockRefIndex) returns BlockPositionColumn
	var column = BlockPositionColumn.create()
	column.resize(count)

	for position in 0 upto count 'each'
		try column.set(position, value: value) otherwise panic("filledPositions: column.set OOB at {position}")
	end 'each'

	return column
end 'filledPositions'

function filledTours(count BlockRefIndex) returns NestTourColumn
	var column = NestTourColumn.create()
	column.resize(count)
	return column
end 'filledTours'

function collectBlockReads(module StdModule, block IrBlock, reads StdValueUseSet)
	for opIdx in block.opRefs 'eachOp'
		collectStdOpUses(module.getOp(opIdx), referenced: reads)
	end 'eachOp'

	if block.hasTerminatorOp() 'terminated'
		collectStdOpUses(module.getOp(block.terminatorIndex()), referenced: reads)
	end 'terminated'

	for edge in block.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
		for argId in edge.argIds 'eachArg'
			reads.insert(argId)
		end 'eachArg'
	end 'eachEdge'

	for arg in block.blockArgs 'eachPhi'
		reads.insert(arg.valueId)
	end 'eachPhi'
end 'collectBlockReads'

// Splice every admissible called-once callee into its caller, callees before callers.
//
// ⭐ **WAVES, NOT RECURSION.** Each candidate's callees among the candidates are counted; a candidate with
// none left unprocessed is ready, and the ready candidates of one wave are spliced caller by caller in one
// walk of each caller. A candidate never ready when the ready list drains lies on a cycle of candidates,
// or calls into one: the cycle's members are refused and their callers counted down, and the waves go on.
function spliceCalledOnceCallees(project Project, module StdModule, byName FunctionNameIndex, def StdDefinedValues, cloner StdOpCloner, meta StdOpMetaMemo, namedByGolden BoolArray)
	let funcCount = module.funcCount() as FuncIndex
	let census = CalledOnceCensus.take(project, module: module, byName: byName)
	var refusals = OrdinalTally.create(CalledOnceRefusal.allCaseNames.count())
	var candidate = BoolArray.create()
	candidate.resize(funcCount)
	var pendingCallees = SiteCountArray.create()
	pendingCallees.resize(funcCount)
	var processed = BoolArray.create()
	processed.resize(funcCount)
	var ready = FuncIndexArray.create()
	var candidateCount = 0 as FuncIndex
	let framesAreRecorded = targetEmitsInlineFrameRecords(project.target)

	for funcIndex in 0 upto funcCount 'eachFunc'
		if not census.isCalledOnce(funcIndex) 'notCalledOnce'
			continue
		end 'notCalledOnce'

		let func = module.getFunc(funcIndex)

		if not framesAreRecorded 'noRecordsOnThisTarget'
			noteCalledOnceRefusal(refusals, reason: CalledOnceRefusal.noInlineFrameRecords, func: func)
			continue
		end 'noRecordsOnThisTarget'

		if isGoldenNamed(namedByGolden, funcIndex: funcIndex) 'aGoldenWantsTheBody'
			noteCalledOnceRefusal(refusals, reason: CalledOnceRefusal.namedByGoldenRequest, func: func)
			continue
		end 'aGoldenWantsTheBody'

		if census.callerOfCallee(funcIndex) == funcIndex 'callsItself'
			noteCalledOnceRefusal(refusals, reason: CalledOnceRefusal.recursive, func: func)
			continue
		end 'callsItself'

		if splicingWouldWidenTheSafePoint(func, caller: module.getFunc(census.callerOfCallee(funcIndex))) 'wouldWidenTheSafePoint'
			noteCalledOnceRefusal(refusals, reason: CalledOnceRefusal.safePointProvenance, func: func)
			continue
		end 'wouldWidenTheSafePoint'

		match scanSpliceableShape(module, func: func, meta: meta) 'shape'
			refused(reason) then noteCalledOnceRefusal(refusals, reason: reason, func: func)
			spliceable then admitCandidate(funcIndex, census: census, candidate: candidate, pendingCallees: pendingCallees, candidateCount: candidateCount)
		end 'shape'
	end 'eachFunc'

	if candidateCount == 0 'nothingCalledOnce'
		logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "inlineLeaves: 0 called-once callee(s) spliced; left as calls: {refusals.summary(CalledOnceRefusal.allCaseNames)}")
		return
	end 'nothingCalledOnce'

	for funcIndex in 0 upto funcCount 'seedReady'
		if isCandidate(candidate, funcIndex: funcIndex) and pendingCalleesOf(pendingCallees, funcIndex: funcIndex) == 0 'noCalleeLeft'
			ready.push(funcIndex)
		end 'noCalleeLeft'
	end 'seedReady'

	let budget = hoistPressureBudgetFor(project.target)
	var stats = InlineLeafStats.create()
	var remaining = candidateCount

	// Built ONCE over every candidate — see `ValueOriginTable.indexRowsOfFunctions` for the cost of the
	// alternative — and kept honest across the waves by `copyRows`, which notes the rows each splice appends.
	var origins = project.valueOrigins.indexRowsOfFunctions(candidate, funcCount: funcCount)
	let calleesOfCallers = groupCandidatesByCaller(census, candidate: candidate, funcCount: funcCount)
	var wave = CalledOnceWave.create(funcCount)
	var callerFacts = CallerSiteFactsMap.create()

	// The liveness workspace and the scratch set every caller's facts and every callee's measurement
	// refill — one of each for the whole pass rather than one per function measured.
	var liveness = StdLiveness.create()
	var across = StdValueUseSet.createRecordingIds()

	while remaining > 0 'eachWave'
		if ready.count() == 0 'onlyCyclesLeft'
			remaining = remaining - refuseCandidateCycles(module, census: census, candidate: candidate, processed: processed, pendingCallees: pendingCallees, ready: ready, refusals: refusals)
			continue
		end 'onlyCyclesLeft'

		remaining = remaining - spliceWave(project, module: module, byName: byName, census: census, calleesOfCallers: calleesOfCallers, budget: budget, candidate: candidate, processed: processed, pendingCallees: pendingCallees, ready: ready, refusals: refusals, stats: stats, def: def, cloner: cloner, meta: meta, origins: origins, wave: wave, callerFacts: callerFacts, liveness: liveness, across: across)
	end 'eachWave'

	let spliced = stats.inlinedSites
	let declinedGuards = stats.declinedParamGuards
	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "inlineLeaves: {spliced} called-once callee(s) spliced, {declinedGuards} spliced parameter cascade(s) declined as provably dead; left as calls: {refusals.summary(CalledOnceRefusal.allCaseNames)}")
end 'spliceCalledOnceCallees'

// Caller -> the candidates whose one site it holds, so a caller's facts are taken from the census's
// coordinates rather than by searching the caller for its sites.
function groupCandidatesByCaller(census CalledOnceCensus, candidate BoolArray, funcCount FuncIndex) returns CsrGraph
	var callerCol = CsrColumn.create()
	var calleeCol = CsrColumn.create()

	for calleeIndex in 0 upto funcCount 'eachFunc'
		if isCandidate(candidate, funcIndex: calleeIndex) 'aCandidate'
			callerCol.push(census.callerOfCallee(calleeIndex) as CsrIndex)
			calleeCol.push(calleeIndex as CsrIndex)
		end 'aCandidate'
	end 'eachFunc'

	return buildCsr(callerCol, to: calleeCol, nodeCount: funcCount as CsrIndex)
end 'groupCandidatesByCaller'

// Count `calleeIndex` as a candidate, and as one more callee its caller waits for. `candidateCount` is
// the caller's running total, advanced by reference.
function admitCandidate(calleeIndex FuncIndex, census CalledOnceCensus, candidate BoolArray, pendingCallees SiteCountArray, candidateCount FuncIndex)
	try candidate.set(calleeIndex, value: true) otherwise panic("admitCandidate: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the candidate column")
	let callerIndex = census.callerOfCallee(calleeIndex)
	try pendingCallees.set(callerIndex, value: pendingCalleesOf(pendingCallees, funcIndex: callerIndex) + 1) otherwise panic("admitCandidate: function index {callerIndex} is outside the pending column")
	candidateCount = candidateCount + 1
end 'admitCandidate'

function isGoldenNamed(namedByGolden BoolArray, funcIndex FuncIndex) returns bool
	return try namedByGolden.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("isGoldenNamed: function index {funcIndex} is outside the golden-named column")
end 'isGoldenNamed'

function isCandidate(candidate BoolArray, funcIndex FuncIndex) returns bool
	return try candidate.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("isCandidate: function index {funcIndex} is outside the candidate column")
end 'isCandidate'

function pendingCalleesOf(pendingCallees SiteCountArray, funcIndex FuncIndex) returns SiteCount
	return try pendingCallees.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("pendingCalleesOf: function index {funcIndex} is outside the pending column")
end 'pendingCalleesOf'

function noteCalledOnceRefusal(refusals OrdinalTally, reason CalledOnceRefusal, func IrFunction)
	refusals.add(reason.ordinal, count: 1)
	logTrace(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "inlineLeaves: called-once callee '{String.from(func.name)}' left as a call — {calledOnceRefusalText(reason)}")
end 'noteCalledOnceRefusal'

// One candidate is done, spliced or refused: its caller has one fewer callee to wait for, and joins the
// ready list when that was the last.
function retireCandidate(calleeIndex FuncIndex, census CalledOnceCensus, candidate BoolArray, processed BoolArray, pendingCallees SiteCountArray, ready FuncIndexArray)
	try processed.set(calleeIndex, value: true) otherwise panic("retireCandidate: function index {calleeIndex} is outside the processed column")
	let callerIndex = census.callerOfCallee(calleeIndex)
	let pending = pendingCalleesOf(pendingCallees, funcIndex: callerIndex)

	if pending == 0 'notCounted'
		panic("retireCandidate: '{calleeIndex}' retires against a caller with no pending callee — every candidate was counted at its caller when admitted")
	end 'notCounted'

	try pendingCallees.set(callerIndex, value: pending - 1) otherwise panic("retireCandidate: function index {callerIndex} is outside the pending column")

	if pending == 1 and isCandidate(candidate, funcIndex: callerIndex) 'callerBecomesReady'
		ready.push(callerIndex)
	end 'callerBecomesReady'
end 'retireCandidate'

// The ready list drained with candidates left: every one of them waits on a callee that waits, so at least
// one cycle of candidates exists. Walking each unprocessed candidate up its caller chain finds the cycle it
// leads to or lies on; the members are refused and retired, which frees whatever hangs off them. Answers
// how many candidates it retired.
function refuseCandidateCycles(module StdModule, census CalledOnceCensus, candidate BoolArray, processed BoolArray, pendingCallees SiteCountArray, ready FuncIndexArray, refusals OrdinalTally) returns FuncIndex
	let funcCount = module.funcCount() as FuncIndex
	var walkStamp = FuncIndexArray.create()
	walkStamp.resize(funcCount)
	var onCycle = BoolArray.create()
	onCycle.resize(funcCount)

	// Stamps count from 1 so a zeroed column reads as "never walked".
	for start in 0 upto funcCount 'eachStart'
		if not isCandidate(candidate, funcIndex: start) or isProcessed(processed, funcIndex: start) 'notWaiting'
			continue
		end 'notWaiting'

		markCycleFrom(start, stamp: start + 1, census: census, candidate: candidate, processed: processed, walkStamp: walkStamp, onCycle: onCycle)
	end 'eachStart'

	var refused = 0 as FuncIndex

	// ⛔ EVERY MEMBER LEAVES CANDIDACY BEFORE ANY MEMBER RETIRES. A member's caller is another member, and
	// retiring one makes a caller with no callee left READY if it is still a candidate — so retiring in one
	// pass would make the other members ready, splice each into the other, and copy a body whose value
	// space the wave measured before it grew.
	for funcIndex in 0 upto funcCount 'eachMemberLeaves'
		if isOnCycle(onCycle, funcIndex: funcIndex) 'aMember'
			try candidate.set(funcIndex, value: false) otherwise panic("refuseCandidateCycles: candidate.set OOB at {funcIndex}")
		end 'aMember'
	end 'eachMemberLeaves'

	for funcIndex in 0 upto funcCount 'eachMember'
		if not isOnCycle(onCycle, funcIndex: funcIndex) 'notAMember'
			continue
		end 'notAMember'

		noteCalledOnceRefusal(refusals, reason: CalledOnceRefusal.recursive, func: module.getFunc(funcIndex))
		retireCandidate(funcIndex, census: census, candidate: candidate, processed: processed, pendingCallees: pendingCallees, ready: ready)
		refused = refused + 1
	end 'eachMember'

	if refused == 0 'noCycleFound'
		panic("refuseCandidateCycles: the ready list drained with candidates left and no cycle among them — a candidate with no pending callee was never made ready")
	end 'noCycleFound'

	return refused
end 'refuseCandidateCycles'

// Follow the caller chain from `start` through unprocessed candidates under one stamp; meeting the stamp
// again closes a cycle, and every node from that point back to the meeting one is on it.
function markCycleFrom(start FuncIndex, stamp FuncIndex, census CalledOnceCensus, candidate BoolArray, processed BoolArray, walkStamp FuncIndexArray, onCycle BoolArray)
	var path = FuncIndexArray.create()
	var at = start

	while isCandidate(candidate, funcIndex: at) and not isProcessed(processed, funcIndex: at) 'eachLink'
		let seen = try walkStamp.get(at) otherwise panic("markCycleFrom: walkStamp.get OOB at {at}")

		if seen == stamp 'closesACycle'
			markCycleMembers(path, closingAt: at, onCycle: onCycle)
			return
		end 'closesACycle'

		if seen != 0 'joinsAnEarlierWalk'
			return
		end 'joinsAnEarlierWalk'

		try walkStamp.set(at, value: stamp) otherwise panic("markCycleFrom: walkStamp.set OOB at {at}")
		path.push(at)
		at = census.callerOfCallee(at)
	end 'eachLink'
end 'markCycleFrom'

function markCycleMembers(path FuncIndexArray, closingAt FuncIndex, onCycle BoolArray)
	var inside = false

	for member in path 'eachOnPath'
		inside = inside or member == closingAt

		if inside 'onTheCycle'
			try onCycle.set(member, value: true) otherwise panic("markCycleMembers: onCycle.set OOB at {member}")
		end 'onTheCycle'
	end 'eachOnPath'
end 'markCycleMembers'

function isOnCycle(onCycle BoolArray, funcIndex FuncIndex) returns bool
	return try onCycle.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("isOnCycle: onCycle.get OOB at {funcIndex}")
end 'isOnCycle'

function isProcessed(processed BoolArray, funcIndex FuncIndex) returns bool
	return try processed.get(funcIndex) otherwise panic("isProcessed: function index {funcIndex} is outside the processed column")
end 'isProcessed'

// One wave: every ready candidate is checked against the pressure budget at its site, the admitted ones
// are spliced caller by caller, and all of them are retired. Answers how many candidates it retired.
function spliceWave(project Project, module StdModule, byName FunctionNameIndex, census CalledOnceCensus, calleesOfCallers CsrGraph, budget HoistPressureBudget, candidate BoolArray, processed BoolArray, pendingCallees SiteCountArray, ready FuncIndexArray, refusals OrdinalTally, stats InlineLeafStats, def StdDefinedValues, cloner StdOpCloner, meta StdOpMetaMemo, origins ValueOriginRowIndex, wave CalledOnceWave, callerFacts CallerSiteFactsMap, liveness StdLiveness, across StdValueUseSet) returns FuncIndex
	var retiring = FuncIndexArray.create()
	var calleesByCaller = CalleesByCallerMap.create()

	// The ready list is consumed whole, then refilled by the retirements below — a caller made ready by
	// this wave's splices belongs to the next.
	for calleeIndex in ready 'eachReady'
		retiring.push(calleeIndex)
		groupByCaller(calleesByCaller, callerIndex: census.callerOfCallee(calleeIndex), calleeIndex: calleeIndex)
	end 'eachReady'

	ready.clear()
	var callers = FuncIndexArray.create()

	for (callerIndex, callees) in calleesByCaller 'eachCaller'
		let caller = module.getFunc(callerIndex)
		let facts = siteFactsOf(callerFacts, module: module, caller: caller, callerIndex: callerIndex, census: census, calleesOfCallers: calleesOfCallers, byName: byName, liveness: liveness, across: across)
		var admittedAny = false

		for calleeIndex in callees 'eachCandidate'
			let callee = module.getFunc(calleeIndex)
			let valueBound = calleeValueBound(callerFacts, module: module, callee: callee, calleeIndex: calleeIndex)

			if fitsSpliceBudget(module, budget: budget, facts: facts, caller: caller, callee: callee, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, calleeValueBound: valueBound, across: across, liveness: liveness) 'admitted'
				wave.admit(calleeIndex, valueBound: valueBound, blockCount: callee.blockRefs.count())
				admittedAny = true
			end 'admitted' else 'overBudget'
				noteCalledOnceRefusal(refusals, reason: CalledOnceRefusal.pressureBudgetExceeded, func: callee)
			end 'overBudget'
		end 'eachCandidate'

		if admittedAny 'somethingToSplice'
			callers.push(callerIndex)
		end 'somethingToSplice'
	end 'eachCaller'

	let readyCount = retiring.count()
	let callerCount = calleesByCaller.count()
	logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "inlineLeaves: called-once wave — {readyCount} callee(s) ready across {callerCount} caller(s)")

	let admission = SpliceAdmission.calledOnce(wave)

	for callerIndex in callers 'eachSplicingCaller'
		// A callee is ready only once every callee of its own is processed, so a body copied this wave is
		// never also a body grown this wave — which is what keeps the value bound the wave measured for it
		// true at the copy (`buildValueColumn`). Asserted: a ready list built any other way breaks it silently.
		if wave.admits(callerIndex) 'bothSidesOfOneWave'
			panic("spliceWave: '{String.from(module.getFunc(callerIndex).name)}' receives a splice in the wave that also splices it away — a callee must be processed before its caller is ready, and this one was made ready while still pending")
		end 'bothSidesOfOneWave'

		let facts = try callerFacts.get(callerIndex) otherwise panic("spliceWave: no site facts for a caller whose callees this wave admitted — the admission loop above takes them first")
		var splicer = SiteSplicer.create(project, module: module, func: module.getFunc(callerIndex), funcIndex: callerIndex, byName: byName, admission: admission, origins: origins, stats: stats, def: def, cloner: cloner, meta: meta, ids: facts.ids, seeded: true)
		splicer.spliceCalledOnceSites(facts, wave: wave)
	end 'eachSplicingCaller'

	for calleeIndex in retiring 'eachRetired'
		// The body MOVED: its one site now holds the copy and nothing reaches the original, which
		// `eliminateDeadFunctions` drops before isel. Every pass between here and there walks every
		// function's blocks, so the original is emptied now rather than carried as a second copy of
		// itself through the rest of the pipeline.
		if wave.admits(calleeIndex) 'spliced'
			var spliced = module.getFunc(calleeIndex)
			spliced.blockRefs = BlockRefArray.create()
			spliced.bodySplicedAway = true
		end 'spliced'

		wave.forget(calleeIndex)
		retireCandidate(calleeIndex, census: census, candidate: candidate, processed: processed, pendingCallees: pendingCallees, ready: ready)
	end 'eachRetired'

	return retiring.count() as FuncIndex
end 'spliceWave'

// Caller -> the wave's ready callees whose one site it holds.
typealias CalleesByCallerMap = Map with (FuncIndex, FuncIndexArray)

function groupByCaller(calleesByCaller CalleesByCallerMap, callerIndex FuncIndex, calleeIndex FuncIndex)
	if var known = try calleesByCaller.get(callerIndex) 'grouped'
		known.push(calleeIndex)
		return
	end 'grouped'

	var callees = FuncIndexArray.create()
	callees.push(calleeIndex)
	calleesByCaller.upsert(callerIndex, value: callees)
end 'groupByCaller'

// ⭐ **THE PRESSURE BUDGET — the unswitcher's, asked of the splice's two halves.** After the splice, the
// caller's values live across the site are live through the callee's whole body, on top of whatever the
// body holds at its own busiest point: its largest loop-header live-in set, or its largest block live-in
// when it has no loop. A sum past the allocatable GPRs less the margin is a hot spill or an E5001 the
// caller did not have before, so the callee stays a call.
//
// Both halves are measured the way `unswitchInvariantGuards.estimateLiveAcross` measures a loop's: a
// value counts where a LOOP reads it. On the caller's side that is the loop around the site — a value
// live through it that nothing in it reads is a slot the allocator fills once outside the loop, never a
// register inside it — and with no loop around the site every value live across it is read later and
// counts. A parameter live at the body's busiest point IS the caller's argument after the splice, so it
// is counted on the caller's side when that argument already counts there and on the body's side
// otherwise.
function fitsSpliceBudget(module StdModule, budget HoistPressureBudget, facts CallerSiteFacts, caller IrFunction, callee IrFunction, calleeIndex FuncIndex, calleeValueBound ValueId, across StdValueUseSet, liveness StdLiveness) returns bool
	match budget 'b'
		unbounded then return true
		registers(count) then return spliceFitsRegisters(module, facts: facts, caller: caller, callee: callee, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, calleeValueBound: calleeValueBound, budget: count, across: across, liveness: liveness)
	end 'b'
end 'fitsSpliceBudget'

// `across` is the caller's scratch set, refilled per site from the list the caller's facts hold.
//
// ⭐ **THE BODY IS MEASURED ONLY WHERE THE ANSWER TURNS ON IT.** Its count is never negative, so a site
// already over the budget refuses before the body is read; and it is bounded by the body's own value count,
// so a body too small to reach the budget from the site's count admits before it is read. Both answer
// exactly what the full measurement would, and between them a liveness over a copied body is asked only of
// the callees whose answer is not already known.
function spliceFitsRegisters(module StdModule, facts CallerSiteFacts, caller IrFunction, callee IrFunction, calleeIndex FuncIndex, calleeValueBound ValueId, budget RegCount, across StdValueUseSet, liveness StdLiveness) returns bool
	let site = facts.siteOf(calleeIndex, callee: callee)
	let acrossCount = site.across.count() as RegCount

	if acrossCount > budget 'overBeforeTheBody'
		logTrace(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "inlineLeaves: splicing '{String.from(callee.name)}' into '{String.from(caller.name)}' holds {acrossCount} value(s) across the site that its loop reads, over the budget of {budget} before the body is counted")
		return false
	end 'overBeforeTheBody'

	across.clear()

	for valueId in site.across 'eachAcross'
		across.insert(valueId)
	end 'eachAcross'

	let bodyBound = bodyLiveInBound(callee, valueBound: calleeValueBound, args: site.args, across: across)

	if acrossCount + bodyBound <= budget 'fitsWhateverTheBodyHolds'
		logTrace(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "inlineLeaves: splicing '{String.from(callee.name)}' into '{String.from(caller.name)}' holds {acrossCount} value(s) across the site that its loop reads and at most {bodyBound} more anywhere in the body, against a budget of {budget}")
		return true
	end 'fitsWhateverTheBodyHolds'

	let bodyCount = largestBodyLiveIn(module, func: callee, valueBound: calleeValueBound, args: site.args, across: across, liveness: liveness)
	logTrace(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "inlineLeaves: splicing '{String.from(callee.name)}' into '{String.from(caller.name)}' holds {acrossCount} value(s) across the site that its loop reads and {bodyCount} more at the body's busiest point, against a budget of {budget}")
	return acrossCount + bodyCount <= budget
end 'spliceFitsRegisters'

// One past the highest id a callee's body uses — the width of the column its copy needs and the value
// space its liveness is solved over. A callee that was itself spliced into holds the answer in its own
// facts: its minter is re-seeded to one past the highest id its last wave's copies defined, which is
// exactly what a walk of its ops would find. Every other callee is walked.
function calleeValueBound(callerFacts CallerSiteFactsMap, module StdModule, callee IrFunction, calleeIndex FuncIndex) returns ValueId
	if let facts = try callerFacts.get(calleeIndex) 'wasACaller'
		return facts.ids.nextId()
	end 'wasACaller'

	return nextFreeValueId(module, func: callee)
end 'calleeValueBound'

// The caller's site facts, computed the first wave it is spliced into and kept for the rest.
function siteFactsOf(callerFacts CallerSiteFactsMap, module StdModule, caller IrFunction, callerIndex FuncIndex, census CalledOnceCensus, calleesOfCallers CsrGraph, byName FunctionNameIndex, liveness StdLiveness, across StdValueUseSet) returns CallerSiteFacts
	if let known = try callerFacts.get(callerIndex) 'known'
		return known
	end 'known'

	let facts = CallerSiteFacts.of(module, func: caller, callerIndex: callerIndex, census: census, calleesOfCallers: calleesOfCallers, byName: byName, liveness: liveness, across: across)
	callerFacts.upsert(callerIndex, value: facts)
	return facts
end 'siteFactsOf'

// An upper bound on `largestBodyLiveIn`'s answer that costs no liveness: a block's live-in set holds
// distinct values, every one of which is a body value or a parameter, so no block can hold more than the
// body's whole value space less the parameters the caller already holds across the site.
function bodyLiveInBound(func IrFunction, valueBound ValueId, args ValueIdArray, across StdValueUseSet) returns RegCount
	var count = (valueBound - valueIdAtParamPosition(func.paramCount)) as RegCount

	for arg in args 'eachArg'
		if not across.contains(arg) 'diesAtTheCall'
			count = count + 1
		end 'diesAtTheCall'
	end 'eachArg'

	return count
end 'bodyLiveInBound'

// The body's busiest point: its largest loop-header live-in set, or its largest block live-in when it
// holds no loop — less the parameters whose argument the caller already holds across the site.
//
// A one-block body is answered without a liveness: every value it holds is defined in that block, so
// nothing is live into it, loop header or not.
function largestBodyLiveIn(module StdModule, func IrFunction, valueBound ValueId, args ValueIdArray, across StdValueUseSet, liveness StdLiveness) returns RegCount
	let blockCount = func.blockRefs.count()

	if blockCount == 1 'everyValueDefinedInTheOneBlock'
		return 0
	end 'everyValueDefinedInTheOneBlock'

	liveness.recompute(module, func: func, valueBound: valueBound)
	let loops = NaturalLoops.find(liveness.succ, nodeCount: blockCount as CsrIndex)
	var liveIn = ValueIdArray.create()
	var largest = 0 as RegCount

	if loops.headers.count() == 0 'loopless'
		for position in 0 upto blockCount 'eachBlock'
			largest = maxRegCount(largest, other: bodyLiveInBeyondTheSite(liveness, position: position, paramCount: func.paramCount, args: args, across: across, liveIn: liveIn))
		end 'eachBlock'

		return largest
	end 'loopless'

	for header in loops.headers 'eachHeader'
		largest = maxRegCount(largest, other: bodyLiveInBeyondTheSite(liveness, position: header as BlockRefIndex, paramCount: func.paramCount, args: args, across: across, liveIn: liveIn))
	end 'eachHeader'

	return largest
end 'largestBodyLiveIn'

// The values live into one body block that the caller does not already hold across the site: every
// non-parameter, and every parameter whose argument dies at the call.
function bodyLiveInBeyondTheSite(liveness StdLiveness, position BlockRefIndex, paramCount ParamCount, args ValueIdArray, across StdValueUseSet, liveIn ValueIdArray) returns RegCount
	liveness.collectLiveIn(position, out: liveIn)
	var count = 0 as RegCount

	for valueId in liveIn 'eachLive'
		if valueId >= valueIdAtParamPosition(paramCount) 'aBodyValue'
			count = count + 1
			continue
		end 'aBodyValue'

		let arg = try args.get(paramPositionOfValueId(valueId)) otherwise panic("bodyLiveInBeyondTheSite: parameter {valueId} has no argument among the site's {args.count()} — the arity check refuses a mismatch before any splice")

		if not across.contains(arg) 'diesAtTheCall'
			count = count + 1
		end 'diesAtTheCall'
	end 'eachLive'

	return count
end 'bodyLiveInBeyondTheSite'

function maxRegCount(a RegCount, other RegCount) returns RegCount
	return a if a >= other else other
end 'maxRegCount'

// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
// The splice
// ══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

// Which callees one splicer run admits, and the value-space width each admitted callee's copy needs.
union SpliceAdmission
	leaves(plan LeafPlan)
	calledOnce(wave CalledOnceWave)
end 'SpliceAdmission'

// Per-caller splicer: owns the function it rewrites plus the fresh-ValueId counter every copied body
// draws from, seeded past every id the function already uses.
type SiteSplicer
	var project as Project
	var module as StdModule
	var func as IrFunction
	var funcIndex as FuncIndex
	var byName as FunctionNameIndex
	var admission as SpliceAdmission
	var origins as ValueOriginRowIndex
	var stats as InlineLeafStats

	// The function's fresh-`ValueId` counter. The called-once rule hands in the caller's own, kept across
	// waves (`CallerSiteFacts.ids`); the leaf rule hands in the shared unseeded one, SEEDED LAZILY as a
	// cost decision — `nextFreeValueId` walks every block arg and every live op of the function, while that
	// rule constructs one of these per function in the module. `InlineManagedPrimitives.ids` carries the
	// same shape and the same measurement.
	var ids as ValueMinter
	var seeded as bool

	// The highest id some op or phi of the function DEFINES, kept as the copies mint: the minter hands
	// out an id for every index of a callee's value space, and an index no copied op defines — a value in
	// a declined cascade, or an op after an aborting one — leaves a gap the next wave's seed must not sit
	// above, since a seed taken from the function's ops would not. Read only by the called-once rule.
	var highestDefined as ValueId

	// The current block's sites, in ascending position, and the continuation each split minted, in the
	// same order. Reused across the function's blocks (truncated, not reallocated) and shared-empty until
	// the first site — see `sharedEmptySitePositions`.
	var sitePositions as BlockOpPosArray
	var siteCallees as FuncIndexArray
	var contPositions as BlockPositionColumn
	var siteBuffersSeeded as bool

	// The def enumerator's sink, the op cloner and the variant memo, shared by every function's splices:
	// this type is constructed per function.
	var def as StdDefinedValues
	var cloner as StdOpCloner
	var meta as StdOpMetaMemo

	static function create(project Project, module StdModule, func IrFunction, funcIndex FuncIndex, byName FunctionNameIndex, admission SpliceAdmission, origins ValueOriginRowIndex, stats InlineLeafStats, def StdDefinedValues, cloner StdOpCloner, meta StdOpMetaMemo, ids ValueMinter, seeded bool) returns SiteSplicer
		return Self{
			project: project,
			module: module,
			func: func,
			funcIndex: funcIndex,
			byName: byName,
			admission: admission,
			origins: origins,
			stats: stats,
			ids: ids,
			seeded: seeded,
			highestDefined: 0,
			sitePositions: sharedEmptySitePositions,
			siteCallees: sharedEmptySiteCallees,
			contPositions: sharedEmptyContPositions,
			siteBuffersSeeded: false,
			def: def,
			cloner: cloner,
			meta: meta
		}
	end 'create'

	// The function's fresh-ValueId counter, seeded past every id it already uses on first use.
	function valueIds() returns ValueMinter
		if self.seeded 'already'
			return self.ids
		end 'already'

		self.ids = ValueMinter.resuming(nextFreeValueId(self.module, func: self.func))
		self.seeded = true
		return self.ids
	end 'valueIds'

	function seedSiteBuffers()
		if self.siteBuffersSeeded 'already'
			return
		end 'already'

		self.sitePositions = BlockOpPosArray.create()
		self.siteCallees = FuncIndexArray.create()
		self.contPositions = BlockPositionColumn.create()
		self.siteBuffersSeeded = true
	end 'seedSiteBuffers'

	function clearSiteBuffers()
		self.seedSiteBuffers()
		self.sitePositions.truncate(0)
		self.siteCallees.truncate(0)
		self.contPositions.truncate(0)
	end 'clearSiteBuffers'

	// ⭐ **THE BOUND IS SNAPSHOTTED, AND THAT IS WHAT KEEPS THIS LOOP OFF ITS OWN OUTPUT — and, unlike
	// `InlineManagedPrimitives`, nothing is revisited at all.** A splice appends two kinds of block and
	// neither holds a site this run admits: a COPIED body holds no call under the leaf rule, and under the
	// called-once rule every called-once callee inside it was spliced in an earlier wave; a CONTINUATION
	// holds ops of the ORIGINAL block, every site in which `inlineSitesIn` has already collected before
	// it split anything. So one pass over the original blocks IS the whole walk — no worklist, no
	// "already expanded" marker column, and no chain to follow.
	function run()
		let originalBlockCount = self.func.blockRefs.count()

		for i in 0 upto originalBlockCount 'eachBlock'
			let blockRef = try self.func.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.run: blockRefs.get OOB at {i} despite the snapshot bound")
			self.inlineSitesIn(blockRef)
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'run'

	// ⭐⭐ **ONE BLOCK, ONE FORWARD WALK, THEN A BACKWARD SPLIT PER SITE — never carve-then-rewalk.**
	// Splitting FORWARD hands each continuation the whole remaining tail and copies it again at the next
	// site: K sites in a B-op block cost Σ(B − pₖ) ≈ K·B/2, the product term `splitBlockInPlace`'s own
	// header names. Backwards, the k-th split moves exactly the ops between site k and site k+1, and the
	// total is B − p₀ — linear, whatever K is.
	//
	// ⭐ The block's EXIT lands on the LAST continuation for free rather than by a special case: the
	// first (rightmost) split takes the terminator, the conditional-branch slot and the outgoing edge
	// args, and every later split takes the `unset` that one left behind — which is exactly the
	// un-terminated state each intermediate continuation should be in when its own splice terminates it.
	function inlineSitesIn(blockRef BlockRef)
		if self.collectSites(blockRef) == 0 'noSite'
			return
		end 'noSite'

		self.spliceRecordedSites(blockRef)
	end 'inlineSitesIn'

	// ⭐ **THE CALLED-ONCE RULE'S WALK IS THE CALLER'S SITE TABLE, NOT ITS BLOCKS.** Every candidate site
	// of the caller was located once (`CallerSiteFacts`), so a wave splices the sites this wave admits —
	// in block order, ascending within a block, exactly the order a walk would meet them — and moves the
	// coordinates of the sites it leaves for a later wave. `main` is a caller in many waves, and a walk
	// of it per wave is the whole program per wave.
	function spliceCalledOnceSites(facts CallerSiteFacts, wave CalledOnceWave)
		self.highestDefined = self.valueIds().nextId() - 1
		var plan = SiteAcrossArray.create()

		for site in facts.sites 'eachSite'
			if wave.admits(site.calleeIndex) 'admittedThisWave'
				insertByCoordinates(plan, site: site)
			end 'admittedThisWave'
		end 'eachSite'

		var first = 0 as SiteCount

		while first < plan.count() 'eachBlock'
			let position = plannedSiteAt(plan, index: first).position
			self.clearSiteBuffers()
			var next = first

			while next < plan.count() and plannedSiteAt(plan, index: next).position == position 'eachSiteOfTheBlock'
				var site = plannedSiteAt(plan, index: next)

				if site.spliced 'twice'
					panic("SiteSplicer.spliceCalledOnceSites: the site of function {site.calleeIndex} in '{String.from(self.func.name)}' is spliced a second time — a callee is admitted in one wave and forgotten after it")
				end 'twice'

				site.spliced = true
				self.sitePositions.push(site.opPos)
				self.siteCallees.push(site.calleeIndex)
				next = next + 1
			end 'eachSiteOfTheBlock'

			let blockRef = try self.func.blockRefs.get(position) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.spliceCalledOnceSites: block position {position} is outside '{String.from(self.func.name)}', which holds {self.func.blockRefs.count()} block(s)")
			self.spliceRecordedSites(blockRef)
			self.recoordinatePendingSites(facts, position: position)
			first = next
		end 'eachBlock'

		facts.ids = ValueMinter.resuming(self.highestDefined + 1)
	end 'spliceCalledOnceSites'

	// The block at `position` was just split behind every site this wave spliced in it: a site left for a
	// later wave now stands in the continuation of the nearest spliced site before it, at its distance
	// past that site's call; a site ahead of every spliced one has not moved.
	function recoordinatePendingSites(facts CallerSiteFacts, position BlockRefIndex)
		for i in 0 upto facts.sites.count() 'eachSite'
			var site = plannedSiteAt(facts.sites, index: i)

			if site.spliced or site.position != position 'notLeftInThisBlock'
				continue
			end 'notLeftInThisBlock'

			var k = self.sitePositions.count()

			while k > 0 'eachSplicedSiteBackwards'
				k = k - 1
				let splicedAt = try self.sitePositions.get(k) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.recoordinatePendingSites: sitePositions.get OOB at {k}")

				if splicedAt < site.opPos 'theNearestSplicedSiteBefore'
					site.position = try self.contPositions.get(k) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.recoordinatePendingSites: contPositions.get OOB at {k} — one continuation is recorded per spliced site")
					site.opPos = site.opPos - splicedAt - 1
					break
				end 'theNearestSplicedSiteBefore'
			end 'eachSplicedSiteBackwards'
		end 'eachSite'
	end 'recoordinatePendingSites'

	// The recorded sites of `blockRef` (`sitePositions`/`siteCallees`, ascending), spliced.
	function spliceRecordedSites(blockRef BlockRef)
		let siteCount = self.sitePositions.count()

		// ⛔⛔ **THE MINTER IS SEEDED HERE, BEFORE THE FIRST SPLIT.**
		// `nextFreeValueId` derives its floor from the function's LIVE ops, and every splice below drops
		// a call op out of `opRefs` — so between the drop and the moment the continuation takes that
		// call's result id as a block arg, the function's live set does not contain it. Seeded inside
		// that gap, the minter's floor can sit at or below the result id, and a copied body's fresh
		// value comes back as the very id the continuation is about to define: two definitions of one
		// value, which this tier does not refuse and no golden reads back.
		_ = self.valueIds()

		var contRefs = BlockRefArray.create()
		contRefs.resize(siteCount)
		var k = siteCount

		while k > 0 'eachSiteBackwards'
			k = k - 1
			let at = try self.sitePositions.get(k) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.spliceRecordedSites: sitePositions.get OOB at {k}")

			// Split immediately PAST the call, so the call stays the last op of the block on the left and
			// its continuation begins at the op that followed it.
			let cont = splitBlockInPlace(self.module, func: self.func, head: self.blockAt(blockRef), position: at + 1, label: InlineContLabel)
			try contRefs.set(k, value: registeredBlockRef(self.module, func: self.func, block: cont)) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.spliceRecordedSites: contRefs.set OOB at {k}")
		end 'eachSiteBackwards'

		// The continuations were registered last-site-first, so the i-th site's is the i-th block from the
		// end.
		let blockCount = self.func.blockRefs.count()

		for i in 0 upto siteCount 'eachContinuation'
			self.contPositions.push(blockCount - 1 - i)
		end 'eachContinuation'

		var pred = blockRef

		for i in 0 upto siteCount 'eachSite'
			let contRef = try contRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.spliceRecordedSites: contRefs.get OOB at {i}")
			let calleeIndex = try self.siteCallees.get(i) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.spliceRecordedSites: siteCallees.get OOB at {i}")
			self.spliceCallAtTailOf(pred, contRef: contRef, calleeIndex: calleeIndex)
			pred = contRef
		end 'eachSite'
	end 'spliceRecordedSites'

	// One forward walk of the block, recording every inlinable site and CENSUSING every other direct
	// call. It allocates nothing for a block with no site — which is nearly every block in the module —
	// because the buffers are shared-empty until `considerSite` seeds them.
	//
	// ⚠ The `StdOp` match is EXHAUSTIVE: `call` is named and so is every other variant, so a variant
	// added later fails to compile here until it is consciously placed. A terminator is never a `call`,
	// so the half this walk omits holds no site.
	function collectSites(blockRef BlockRef) returns SiteCount
		if self.siteBuffersSeeded 'reuse'
			self.clearSiteBuffers()
		end 'reuse'

		let block = self.blockAt(blockRef)

		for pos in 0 upto block.opRefs.count() 'eachOp'
			match self.module.getOp(try block.opRefs.get(pos) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.collectSites: opRefs.get OOB at {pos} inside its own count")) 'kind'
				const or
					binOp or
					unaryOp or
					cmp or
					binOpImm or
					cmpImm or
					div or
					mod or
					condBranch or
					branch or
					ret or
					param then break 'kind'
				call(_, callee, _) then self.considerSite(pos, callee: callee)
				retVoid or
					globalAddr or
					loadIndirect or
					storeIndirect or
					osAllocPages or
					osWriteStdout or
					rdataAddr or
					tryCall or
					errorReturn or
					osExit or
					funcAddr or
					callIndirect or
					osPanic or
					osReadClock or
					osProcessSpawn or
					osProcessExitCode or
					osWaitHandles or
					osCloseHandle or
					osWaitHandle or
					osFreePages or
					switch or
					witnessCall or
					osPipeCreate or
					osIocpCreate or
					osEventCreate or
					osThreadCreate or
					osLockInit or
					osLockEnter or
					osLockLeave or
					osReadOverlapped or
					osLastError or
					osProcessSpawnInherit or
					osCancelIo or
					osGetPid or
					osPipeCreateOutbound or
					osWriteFile or
					osReadWallClock or
					witnessTryCall or
					osFileOpenRead or
					osFileClose or
					osReadFile or
					osFileOpenWrite or
					osFileSize or
					osFileDelete or
					osFileRename or
					osFileFindFirst or
					osFileFindClose or
					osFileFindNext or
					osDirCreate or
					osDirCurrentPath or
					osFileAttributes or
					osPanicValue or
					osCommandLine or
					osStdHandle or
					osExecutablePath or
					osWriteStderr or
					stackRecordAddr or
					osOpenNamedSegment or
					osMapNamedSegment or
					osTickCountMs or
					osHandleSetInherit or
					osPipePeek or
					osEnvRead or
					osProcessTerminate or
					osSocketStartup or
					osSocketResolve or
					osSocketFreeAddrInfo or
					osSocketCreate or
					osSocketConnect or
					osSocketSend or
					osSocketRecv or
					osSocketClose or
					osCpuCount or
					osThreadCpuTicks or
					osDecommitPages or
					memFill or
					osCommitPages or
					osReservePages or
					atomicCas or
					atomicRmw or
					currentThreadToken or
					tlsAlloc or
					tlsSetValue or
					tlsSlotLoad or
					osSetEvent or
					osEnterBackgroundPriority then break 'kind'
				osFileOpenWriteExecutable then break 'kind'
				osEnvBlock or
					osEnvBlockFree then break 'kind'
				loadAcquire or
					storeRelease then break 'kind'
				osHandleFileType then break 'kind'
				osCreateNamedSegment or
					osUnmapNamedSegment or
					osUnpublishNamedSegment or
					covPoint or
					osHostSample or
					osNetpoll then break 'kind'
			end 'kind'
		end 'eachOp'

		return self.sitePositions.count()
	end 'collectSites'

	// ⚠ **THE CALLEE IS A RAW `ByteArray` NAME AND IS COMPARED AS BYTES, NEVER PARSED.** Names like
	// `__paramDefault#emitCmp#6` and `<Type>.<method>` reach this tier verbatim, and one of the corpus's
	// own generated names carries a space.
	//
	// A callee the index does not know is a RUNTIME body — `__mm_*`, `__str_*`, `__managed_*`, the
	// synthesized destructor cascades — which are installed AFTER the pipeline and so are absent from
	// `module.functions` at this point. They are therefore never inlined BY CONSTRUCTION rather than by
	// a roster this file would have to keep. (`mrt_panic` is not a call at all: it is an `osPanic` op.)
	function considerSite(pos BlockOpPos, callee ByteArray)
		let calleeIndex = try self.byName.byName.get(callee) otherwise return

		if calleeIndex == self.funcIndex 'selfCall'
			// A self-call cannot be spliced (the body would contain its own copy): the leaf rule has already
			// refused the function for holding a call at all, and the called-once rule for calling itself —
			// so this is counted rather than tested for, and the census says how often.
			self.stats.noteSelfCall()
			return
		end 'selfCall'

		match self.admission 'rule'
			leaves(plan) then self.considerLeafSite(pos, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, plan: plan)
			calledOnce(wave) then self.considerCalledOnceSite(pos, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, wave: wave)
		end 'rule'
	end 'considerSite'

	function considerLeafSite(pos BlockOpPos, calleeIndex FuncIndex, plan LeafPlan)
		match plan.verdictOf(calleeIndex) 'verdict'
			eligible then self.recordLeafSiteUnlessItWidensTheSafePoint(pos, calleeIndex: calleeIndex)
			tooManyOps then self.stats.noteSizeRefusal(plan.isWithinLargerBudget(calleeIndex))
			notALeaf then break 'verdict'
		end 'verdict'
	end 'considerLeafSite'

	// The leaf rule's half of `splicingWouldWidenTheSafePoint`. Asked per SITE rather than per callee: one
	// eligible leaf is spliced at every site it has, and its callers need not agree about provenance.
	function recordLeafSiteUnlessItWidensTheSafePoint(pos BlockOpPos, calleeIndex FuncIndex)
		if splicingWouldWidenTheSafePoint(self.module.getFunc(calleeIndex), caller: self.func) 'wouldWidenTheSafePoint'
			self.stats.noteSafePointRefusal()
			return
		end 'wouldWidenTheSafePoint'

		self.recordSite(pos, calleeIndex: calleeIndex)
	end 'recordLeafSiteUnlessItWidensTheSafePoint'

	function considerCalledOnceSite(pos BlockOpPos, calleeIndex FuncIndex, wave CalledOnceWave)
		if wave.admits(calleeIndex) 'admitted'
			self.recordSite(pos, calleeIndex: calleeIndex)
		end 'admitted'
	end 'considerCalledOnceSite'

	// The value-space width the admitting rule measured for `calleeIndex`, and the block count it saw.
	function valueBoundOf(calleeIndex FuncIndex) returns ValueId
		return match self.admission 'rule'
			leaves(plan) gives plan.valueBoundOf(calleeIndex)
			calledOnce(wave) gives wave.valueBoundOf(calleeIndex)
		end 'rule'
	end 'valueBoundOf'

	function blockCountOf(calleeIndex FuncIndex) returns BlockRefIndex
		return match self.admission 'rule'
			leaves(plan) gives plan.blockCountOf(calleeIndex)
			calledOnce(wave) gives wave.blockCountOf(calleeIndex)
		end 'rule'
	end 'blockCountOf'

	function recordSite(pos BlockOpPos, calleeIndex FuncIndex)
		self.seedSiteBuffers()
		self.sitePositions.push(pos)
		self.siteCallees.push(calleeIndex)
	end 'recordSite'

	// The call is the LAST op of `predRef` by construction — `inlineSitesIn` split immediately past it.
	// It is read, spliced, and then dropped.
	//
	// ⚠ The dropped op is left behind as an ORPHAN in `module.ops`. That is the tier's own model
	// (`IrModule.ops` is append-only and retains what block-rebuilding passes replace), and it is why
	// every reader asks `liveOpIndices` rather than scanning the array.
	function spliceCallAtTailOf(predRef BlockRef, contRef BlockRef, calleeIndex FuncIndex)
		let tail = self.blockAt(predRef).opRefs.count() - 1

		// The splice's own op — the branch into the copied entry — is the CALLER's statement. The clone loop
		// before it re-points the cursor per callee op and leaves it there, so the site's span is CARRIED
		// rather than left on the cursor.
		let siteSpan = self.module.spans.at(self.blockOpRefAt(predRef, pos: tail))
		self.module.spans.current = siteSpan

		match self.opAt(predRef, pos: tail) 'site'
			const or
				binOp or
				unaryOp or
				cmp or
				binOpImm or
				cmpImm or
				div or
				mod or
				condBranch or
				branch or
				ret or
				param then self.refuseNotTheSplitCall()
			call(resultId, callee, args) then self.splice(predRef, contRef: contRef, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, callee: callee, args: args, resultId: resultId, siteSpan: siteSpan)
			retVoid or
				globalAddr or
				loadIndirect or
				storeIndirect or
				osAllocPages or
				osWriteStdout or
				rdataAddr or
				tryCall or
				errorReturn or
				osExit or
				funcAddr or
				callIndirect or
				osPanic or
				osReadClock or
				osProcessSpawn or
				osProcessExitCode or
				osWaitHandles or
				osCloseHandle or
				osWaitHandle or
				osFreePages or
				switch or
				witnessCall or
				osPipeCreate or
				osIocpCreate or
				osEventCreate or
				osThreadCreate or
				osLockInit or
				osLockEnter or
				osLockLeave or
				osReadOverlapped or
				osLastError or
				osProcessSpawnInherit or
				osCancelIo or
				osGetPid or
				osPipeCreateOutbound or
				osWriteFile or
				osReadWallClock or
				witnessTryCall or
				osFileOpenRead or
				osFileClose or
				osReadFile or
				osFileOpenWrite or
				osFileSize or
				osFileDelete or
				osFileRename or
				osFileFindFirst or
				osFileFindClose or
				osFileFindNext or
				osDirCreate or
				osDirCurrentPath or
				osFileAttributes or
				osPanicValue or
				osCommandLine or
				osStdHandle or
				osExecutablePath or
				osWriteStderr or
				stackRecordAddr or
				osOpenNamedSegment or
				osMapNamedSegment or
				osTickCountMs or
				osHandleSetInherit or
				osPipePeek or
				osEnvRead or
				osProcessTerminate or
				osSocketStartup or
				osSocketResolve or
				osSocketFreeAddrInfo or
				osSocketCreate or
				osSocketConnect or
				osSocketSend or
				osSocketRecv or
				osSocketClose or
				osCpuCount or
				osThreadCpuTicks or
				osDecommitPages or
				memFill or
				osCommitPages or
				osReservePages or
				atomicCas or
				atomicRmw or
				currentThreadToken or
				tlsAlloc or
				tlsSetValue or
				tlsSlotLoad or
				osSetEvent or
				osEnterBackgroundPriority then self.refuseNotTheSplitCall()
			osFileOpenWriteExecutable then self.refuseNotTheSplitCall()
			osEnvBlock or
				osEnvBlockFree then self.refuseNotTheSplitCall()
			loadAcquire or
				storeRelease then self.refuseNotTheSplitCall()
			osHandleFileType then self.refuseNotTheSplitCall()
			osCreateNamedSegment or
				osUnmapNamedSegment or
				osUnpublishNamedSegment or
				covPoint or
				osHostSample or
				osNetpoll then self.refuseNotTheSplitCall()
		end 'site'

		var pred = self.blockAt(predRef)
		pred.opRefs.truncate(tail)
		self.stats.noteInlined()
	end 'spliceCallAtTailOf'

	// The backstop the two non-`call` arms above share — ONE site, because they say one thing: the split
	// cut immediately PAST the call, so the call is the last op of the block on the left, and anything
	// else there means a coordinate moved between the split and this read.
	//
	// ⚠ It is a FUNCTION THAT PANICS rather than a function returning the message, because `panic` takes
	// a string LITERAL (E3005 refuses an expression), so a shared message cannot be shared as a value.
	function refuseNotTheSplitCall()
		panic("SiteSplicer.spliceCallAtTailOf: the last op of a spliced block of '{String.from(self.func.name)}' is not the call the split cut past")
	end 'refuseNotTheSplitCall'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE SPLICE.** `predRef` ends in the call (about to be dropped); `contRef` holds everything
	// that followed it, and — if this is the block's last site — the block's whole exit.
	function splice(predRef BlockRef, contRef BlockRef, calleeIndex FuncIndex, callee ByteArray, args ValueIdArray, resultId ValueId, siteSpan SpanId)
		requireCallNamesCallee(callee, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, caller: self.func, byName: self.byName)
		let calleeFunc = self.module.getFunc(calleeIndex)

		if args.count() != calleeFunc.paramCount 'arity'
			panic("SiteSplicer.splice: '{String.from(self.func.name)}' calls '{String.from(callee)}' with {args.count()} argument(s) against {calleeFunc.paramCount} parameter(s) — `lowerCall` emits arguments in declaration order and fills every slot, the trailing layout/witness parameters of a generic callee included, so a mismatch here is a malformed call rather than a shape to decline")
		end 'arity'

		let subst = self.buildValueColumn(calleeFunc, calleeIndex: calleeIndex, args: args)

		// The splice's site nests under whatever site the call's own block carries — the caller's text, or a
		// body an earlier splice copied in.
		let spliceSite = self.func.recordInlineSite(callee, parent: self.blockAt(predRef).inlineSite)
		let blocks = self.mintCopiedBlocks(calleeFunc, guards: self.declinedGuards(calleeFunc, args: args), spliceSite: spliceSite)
		self.attachContinuationArg(contRef, calleeFunc: calleeFunc, resultId: resultId)
		self.copyBodies(calleeFunc, contRef: contRef, blocks: blocks, subst: subst)
		self.project.valueOrigins.copyRows(self.origins, fromFunc: calleeIndex, toFunc: self.funcIndex, subst: subst, calleeParamCount: calleeFunc.paramCount)

		// The head's own exit was moved onto the continuation by the split, so it is un-terminated here
		// and carries no edge. The copied entry block takes no block args (`scanLeafBody` refuses a
		// callee whose entry carries phis), so this branch carries none either.
		//
		// ⚠ The cursor is where the body clone above left it — on a CALLEE op — so the site's own span is
		// restored first: this branch is the caller's call statement, not any line inside the callee.
		self.module.spans.current = siteSpan
		self.module.setTerminator(self.blockAt(predRef), op: StdOp.branch(blocks.targetOf(self.calleeEntryBlockId(calleeFunc))))
	end 'splice'

	// ⭐ **THE DENSE VALUE COLUMN, TOTAL OVER THE CALLEE'S WHOLE ID SPACE — parameters to the site's
	// arguments, everything else to a fresh caller id.**
	//
	// ⭐⭐ **IT MINTS FOR EVERY NON-PARAMETER INDEX, NOT ONLY FOR THE VALUES SOME OP DEFINES, AND THAT IS
	// WHAT MAKES IT TOTAL RATHER THAN NEARLY TOTAL.** There is no unbound id to read, so
	// `cloneStdOp` can look every value up STRICTLY and a column built against the wrong function
	// is a panic instead of a silently wrong operand. The price is the handful of ids that go to values
	// in a declined cascade, or to a gap an earlier pass's orphaned op left in the numbering — a couple
	// per splice, against a caller value space the register allocator sizes its columns by.
	//
	// ⚠ Parameters occupy `0..paramCount-1` BY CONVENTION (`IrValueId.maxon`, `emitParams`), which is why
	// this is a range rather than a scan for `param` ops. `copyBlockOps` CHECKS each `param` op against
	// it as it skips the op, so the convention is verified where it is spent rather than assumed here.
	function buildValueColumn(calleeFunc IrFunction, calleeIndex FuncIndex, args ValueIdArray) returns ValueIdArray
		let bound = self.valueBoundOf(calleeIndex)

		// The admission measured pressure over this body and sized its column by it; a body that grew since
		// is not the one that was admitted. Every splice adds blocks, so the block count is the tripwire —
		// asked in O(1) where a fresh `nextFreeValueId` would walk the body a second time per copy.
		if calleeFunc.blockRefs.count() != self.blockCountOf(calleeIndex) 'theBodyGrewSinceAdmission'
			panic("SiteSplicer.buildValueColumn: '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' holds {calleeFunc.blockRefs.count()} block(s) but was admitted with {self.blockCountOf(calleeIndex)} — something spliced into it between its admission and this copy")
		end 'theBodyGrewSinceAdmission'

		var subst = ValueIdArray.create()
		subst.resize(bound)

		for i in 0 upto calleeFunc.paramCount 'eachParam'
			let arg = try args.get(i) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.buildValueColumn: args.get OOB at {i} despite the arity check")
			try subst.set(i, value: arg) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.buildValueColumn: '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' declares {calleeFunc.paramCount} parameter(s) but its value space is only {bound} wide — parameters occupy ids 0..paramCount-1 by the Std ABI's convention")
		end 'eachParam'

		for v in valueIdAtParamPosition(calleeFunc.paramCount) upto bound 'eachBodyValue'
			try subst.set(v, value: self.valueIds().mint()) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.buildValueColumn: subst.set OOB at {v}")
		end 'eachBodyValue'

		return subst
	end 'buildValueColumn'

	// ⭐⭐ **THE CASCADES THIS SITE DECLINES.** Empty for every callee with no narrowed parameter, which is
	// almost all of them — and that empty `paramGuards` list is what keeps the cost off the common splice:
	// the loop below runs zero times and no proof is asked.
	function declinedGuards(calleeFunc IrFunction, args ValueIdArray) returns DeclinedGuards
		if calleeFunc.paramGuards.isEmpty() 'noNarrowedParameter'
			return sharedDeclinesNothing
		end 'noNarrowedParameter'

		var declined = DeclinedGuards.create(calleeFunc.nextBlockId, guardCount: calleeFunc.paramGuards.count())

		for guard in calleeFunc.paramGuards 'eachGuard'
			if not self.callerProvesArg(args, guard: guard) 'notProvenHere'
				continue
			end 'notProvenHere'

			declined.decline(guard)
			self.stats.noteDeclinedParamGuard()
		end 'eachGuard'

		return declined
	end 'declinedGuards'

	// ⭐⭐ **DOES *THIS* FRAME ALREADY KNOW THE ARGUMENT IS IN THE CALLEE'S RANGE? — THE TWO PROOFS, AND
	// THERE ARE EXACTLY TWO.** Both are compared with the containment every range door in the compiler
	// already uses (`TypeRules`), so this pass reaches no verdict of its own and cannot come to disagree
	// with the guard it is declining.
	function callerProvesArg(args ValueIdArray, guard RangeCheckParamGuard) returns bool
		let arg = try args.get(guard.index) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.callerProvesArg: a callee guarding parameter {guard.index} is called from '{String.from(self.func.name)}' with {args.count()} argument(s) — the arity check has already refused a mismatch")
		return self.callerParamProves(arg, alias: guard.alias) or self.provenRangeProves(arg, alias: guard.alias)
	end 'callerProvesArg'

	// **PROOF ONE: THE ARGUMENT IS ONE OF THIS FUNCTION'S OWN NARROWED PARAMETERS.** Its cascade stands at
	// this function's entry, which dominates every call the body makes, so the value in hand has already
	// passed a range — and if that range lies inside the callee's, the callee's cascade cannot fail either.
	// This is the shape EC6's survey measured at 3 of `regMaskContains`' 14 sites (`hintIsTakeable`
	// forwarding a `RegNum` parameter to two more calls, and `assertOneCallClobber`).
	//
	// ⚠ **A REASSIGNED PARAMETER IS EXCLUDED, AND IT IS NOT A CORNER CASE.** `reassignedParamMask` names
	// the parameters that arrive as a CELL ADDRESS (PBR-2): the range describes what the cell HOLDS, the
	// body may store a new value into it, and the entry guard reads THROUGH the cell rather than checking
	// the pointer (`GuardedOperand.pointeeOf`). The id would still match here, and matching it would spend
	// a proof about the cell's arriving contents on whatever the body last wrote into it.
	//
	// ⚠ A LINEAR SCAN over this function's own cascades, which is one per narrowed parameter and never
	// more than its arity — the bound `entryGuardAlreadyCovers` and `paramAliasOf` already carry.
	function callerParamProves(arg ValueId, alias RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
		if arg >= valueIdAtParamPosition(self.func.paramCount) 'notAParameterOfThisFrame'
			return false
		end 'notAParameterOfThisFrame'

		let position = paramPositionOfValueId(arg)

		if maskHasParam(self.func.reassignedParamMask, param: position) 'arrivesAsACellAddress'
			return false
		end 'arrivesAsACellAddress'

		for ownGuard in self.func.paramGuards 'eachOwnGuard'
			if ownGuard.index == position 'thisParameter'
				return rangeCoversRange(alias, source: ownGuard.alias)
			end 'thisParameter'
		end 'eachOwnGuard'

		return false
	end 'callerParamProves'

	// **PROOF TWO: SOMETHING RECORDED AN INTERVAL FOR IT (EC7 layer 1).** `for r in 0 upto RegisterFileSize`
	// gives its counter a range nothing NAMES — as does a `.count()` result, which is no loop's counter —
	// which the parser records against this
	// function's own `ValueId` numbering and `LowerMaxonToStd` carries across verbatim (that pass does not
	// renumber). It is the proof the four hottest `regMaskContains` sites need and the one no declared type
	// can supply — the counter's written type is the bare `int` its bounds were written at.
	//
	// ⚠ **THE PROBE IS MADE HERE, BEHIND A CALLEE THAT ACTUALLY GUARDS A PARAMETER**, and not hoisted into
	// the per-caller constructor: a `SiteSplicer` is built for every function in the module and nearly none
	// of them splices anything that guards anything, so a probe up there would be one map lookup per
	// function for an answer almost nobody spends.
	function provenRangeProves(arg ValueId, alias RangedTypeAlias) returns bool
		let checks = try self.project.rangeChecks.get(self.func.name) otherwise return false
		let proven = try provenIntRangeOf(checks.provenValueRanges, value: arg) otherwise return false
		return rangeCoversIntBounds(alias, low: proven.low, high: proven.high)
	end 'provenRangeProves'

	function calleeEntryBlockId(calleeFunc IrFunction) returns BlockId
		let entryRef = try calleeFunc.blockRefs.get(0) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.calleeEntryBlockId: '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' has no entry block, which `scanLeafBody` refuses")
		let entry = try self.module.blocks.get(entryRef) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.calleeEntryBlockId: entry blockRef {entryRef} of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' is not in module.blocks")
		return entry.id
	end 'calleeEntryBlockId'

	// Mint one fresh caller block per callee block outside a declined cascade, filling the block column and
	// recording each new block's ref in callee order. Every copy carries the site the callee block's site
	// maps to under this splice.
	function mintCopiedBlocks(calleeFunc IrFunction, guards DeclinedGuards, spliceSite InlineSiteRef) returns SpliceBlocks
		var blocks = SpliceBlocks.create(calleeFunc.nextBlockId, guards: guards, spliceSite: spliceSite)
		blocks.mapCalleeSites(calleeFunc, caller: self.func)
		let entryId = guards.entryFrom(self.calleeEntryBlockId(calleeFunc))

		for i in 0 upto calleeFunc.blockRefs.count() 'eachBlock'
			let calleeBlockRef = try calleeFunc.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.mintCopiedBlocks: blockRefs.get OOB at {i}")
			let calleeBlock = try self.module.blocks.get(calleeBlockRef) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.mintCopiedBlocks: blockRef {calleeBlockRef} of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' is not in module.blocks")

			// ⭐ **A DECLINED CASCADE'S BLOCKS ARE NOT COPIED** (EC7): its `__rc_panic` is one of them, and a
			// copied panic nothing can reach would be a dead block holding a call to `mrt_panic`.
			if guards.isDeclined(calleeBlock.id) 'insideADeclinedCascade'
				continue
			end 'insideADeclinedCascade'

			// The label marks the block the splice ENTERS, which is the callee's entry only while no cascade
			// in front of it was declined — `__il_body` is a claim about this copy, not about the callee, so
			// it follows the entry rather than position 0.
			let fresh = self.module.addBlock(self.func, label: InlineBodyLabel if calleeBlock.id == entryId else calleeBlock.label, heat: calleeBlock.heat, inlineSite: blocks.siteOf(calleeBlock.inlineSite))
			try blocks.blockSubst.set(calleeBlock.id, value: fresh.id) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.mintCopiedBlocks: block '{String.from(calleeBlock.label)}' has id {calleeBlock.id}, outside '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}'s id space of {calleeFunc.nextBlockId}")
			let freshRef = registeredBlockRef(self.module, func: self.func, block: fresh)
			blocks.copiedRefs.push(freshRef)

			// Coverage follows the code: a copied block's source footprint is the CALLEE block's, which is
			// where its ops were written. `addBlock` pushed an empty entry, parallel to `blocks`.
			self.carryBlockSourceInfo(calleeBlockRef, to: freshRef)
		end 'eachBlock'

		self.mapDeclinedCascadeHeads(calleeFunc, blocks: blocks, guards: guards)
		return blocks
	end 'mintCopiedBlocks'

	// ⭐⭐ **EVERY EDGE INTO A DECLINED CASCADE'S HEAD LANDS ON THE BLOCK THAT CASCADE WOULD HAVE PASSED
	// CONTROL TO, and it is spelled as a `blockSubst` entry rather than as a rewrite of the edges.** The
	// substitution column is what `cloneStdOp` reads for every terminator it clones AND what
	// `targetOf` answers the splice's own entry branch from — so writing the redirect there redirects both,
	// and there is no list of edge kinds to keep in step.
	//
	// The cascade's OTHER blocks keep the `0` a resized column starts with, and nothing reads it: a
	// `__rc_chk` is named only by its cascade's head or by the `__rc_chk` before it, and the `__rc_panic`
	// only by those — every one of them declined, and none of them copied.
	function mapDeclinedCascadeHeads(calleeFunc IrFunction, blocks SpliceBlocks, guards DeclinedGuards)
		if not guards.anyDeclined 'nothingDeclined'
			return
		end 'nothingDeclined'

		for guard in calleeFunc.paramGuards 'eachGuard'
			if not guards.isDeclined(guard.headBlockId) 'stillEmitted'
				continue
			end 'stillEmitted'

			let landing = guards.entryFrom(guard.okBlockId)
			try blocks.blockSubst.set(guard.headBlockId, value: blocks.targetOf(landing)) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.mapDeclinedCascadeHeads: cascade head {guard.headBlockId} of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' is outside its own block-id space")
		end 'eachGuard'
	end 'mapDeclinedCascadeHeads'

	// The value list a return into the continuation carries: the one value for a value-returning callee,
	// and NOTHING for a void one. ONE function feeding both users of that arity — every copied
	// `ret`/`retVoid`'s edge, and the continuation's own block-arg list — so the positional
	// `blockArgs[k] ↔ edge.argIds[k]` pairing cannot be built from two readings of the same return type.
	function returnedValues(calleeFunc IrFunction, valueId ValueId) returns ValueIdArray
		var out = ValueIdArray.create()

		match calleeFunc.returnType 'rt'
			void then break 'rt'
			value then out.push(valueId)
		end 'rt'

		return out
	end 'returnedValues'

	// ⭐ **THE CONTINUATION'S SINGLE BLOCK ARG IS THE CALL'S ORIGINAL RESULT ID**, which is the whole
	// reason no op after the site has to change: every one of them still names the value it always did.
	// Its TYPE is the callee's declared return type — what decides the value's register FILE on x64 and
	// arm64 and its valtype on wasm, so a float-returning leaf's result stays in an XMM.
	//
	// A VOID callee gets none. Its `resultId` is a placeholder the lowering mints and nothing reads (a
	// void call's result is not a value any op can name), and there is no `ret v` in the body to feed a
	// block arg with.
	//
	// A void callee touches nothing here — no array is built for it, which is the difference between
	// "the continuation declares no args" and "it declares an empty list", and this pass makes thousands
	// of continuations.
	function attachContinuationArg(contRef BlockRef, calleeFunc IrFunction, resultId ValueId)
		match calleeFunc.returnType 'rt'
			void then break 'rt'
			value(inner) then attachBlockArgs(self.blockAt(contRef), valueIds: self.returnedValues(calleeFunc, valueId: resultId), argType: inner)
		end 'rt'
	end 'attachContinuationArg'

	// Copy every block's phis, ops and exit into the fresh blocks `mintCopiedBlocks` minted, in the same
	// order and skipping the same declined cascades — so the two walks pair up positionally.
	function copyBodies(calleeFunc IrFunction, contRef BlockRef, blocks SpliceBlocks, subst ValueIdArray)
		var copied = 0 as BlockRefIndex

		for i in 0 upto calleeFunc.blockRefs.count() 'eachBlock'
			let calleeBlockRef = try calleeFunc.blockRefs.get(i) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.copyBodies: blockRefs.get OOB at {i}")

			if blocks.guards.isDeclined(self.blockAt(calleeBlockRef).id) 'insideADeclinedCascade'
				continue
			end 'insideADeclinedCascade'

			let freshRef = try blocks.copiedRefs.get(copied) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.copyBodies: copiedRefs.get OOB at {copied} — `mintCopiedBlocks` and this walk skip the same blocks in the same order")
			copied = copied + 1

			// An ABORTING block never reaches its `ret`, which is there for the callee's arity alone
			// (`appendDeadReturn`). Copied as an edge, it would hand the continuation a dead constant beside
			// the real result, and that merge is a phi — which pins a fresh record: once its call is gone the
			// record is `promoteStackRecords`' to lift, and that pass refuses one reaching a phi. The copy
			// leaves the CALLER the same way instead — here, after `copyBody`'s borrow of the callee block
			// has ended, since the append mutates the module.
			if self.copyBody(calleeBlockRef, freshRef: freshRef, contRef: contRef, subst: subst, blocks: blocks, calleeFunc: calleeFunc) 'aborts'
				appendDeadReturn(self.module, func: self.func, block: self.blockAt(freshRef), ids: self.valueIds())

				// The dead return's constants are the last ids minted, and every one of them is defined.
				self.highestDefined = self.valueIds().nextId() - 1
			end 'aborts'
		end 'eachBlock'
	end 'copyBodies'

	// One block's phis, ops and — unless it aborts — its exit. Answers whether the copy still owes the
	// aborting block's dead return.
	function copyBody(calleeBlockRef BlockRef, freshRef BlockRef, contRef BlockRef, subst ValueIdArray, blocks SpliceBlocks, calleeFunc IrFunction) returns bool
		let calleeBlock = self.blockAt(calleeBlockRef)
		self.copyBlockArgs(calleeBlock, freshRef: freshRef, subst: subst)

		if self.copyBlockOps(calleeBlock, freshRef: freshRef, subst: subst, blocks: blocks, calleeFunc: calleeFunc) 'aborts'
			return true
		end 'aborts'

		self.copyBlockExit(calleeBlock, freshRef: freshRef, contRef: contRef, subst: subst, blocks: blocks, calleeFunc: calleeFunc)
		return false
	end 'copyBody'

	// A copied block's phis, through the value column. Their TYPES come across verbatim — a phi's type is
	// a property of the value it carries, not of the function it is in.
	function copyBlockArgs(calleeBlock IrBlock, freshRef BlockRef, subst ValueIdArray)
		if calleeBlock.blockArgs.count() == 0 'noPhis'
			return
		end 'noPhis'

		var blockArgs = IrBlockArgArray.create()

		for arg in calleeBlock.blockArgs 'each'
			let cloned = clonedValue(arg.valueId, subst: subst)
			self.noteDefined(cloned)
			blockArgs.push(IrBlockArg.create(cloned, argType: arg.argType))
		end 'each'

		var fresh = self.blockAt(freshRef)
		fresh.blockArgs = blockArgs
	end 'copyBlockArgs'

	function noteDefined(valueId ValueId)
		if valueId > self.highestDefined 'higher'
			self.highestDefined = valueId
		end 'higher'
	end 'noteDefined'

	// Answers whether the block ABORTS: the copy stops at an op control never comes back from, since what
	// follows one — the callee's own dead return, present for the callee's arity — never runs and is
	// replaced by the caller's (`copyBodies`).
	function copyBlockOps(calleeBlock IrBlock, freshRef BlockRef, subst ValueIdArray, blocks SpliceBlocks, calleeFunc IrFunction) returns bool
		let fresh = self.blockAt(freshRef)

		for opIdx in calleeBlock.opRefs 'eachOp'
			// ⚠ **THE OP IS READ THROUGH `getOp` AND THE CLONE IS BOUND BEFORE THE APPEND, AND BOTH ARE
			// E3070's DOING.** `self.module.ops.get(...)` inline here BORROWS `module`, and `appendOp`
			// mutates it — which the self-compile refuses (`cannot mutate 'module' via 'appendOp' while it
			// is borrowed`).
			let op = self.module.getOp(opIdx)

			// The dialect's own role names the `param` variant and nothing else, so it answers what
			// `inlineOpRole`'s ninety-arm match would, for every copied op of every spliced body.
			if self.meta.hasRole(op, role: OpRole.param) 'parameterOp'
				assertParamOpMatchesConvention(op, calleeFunc: calleeFunc)
				continue
			end 'parameterOp'

			// ⭐ **THE CLONE KEEPS THE CALLEE'S SPAN, WHICH IS THE WHOLE REASON THE FILE RIDES IN THE ROW.**
			// A span carried per-FUNCTION would report the caller's file for every inlined body, so a
			// debugger could never step into one. Read before the append, for the borrow reason above.
			let calleeSpan = self.module.spans.at(opIdx)
			let aborts = self.meta.hasRole(op, role: OpRole.diverging)
			let cloned = self.cloner.clone(op, valueSubst: subst, blockSubst: blocks.blockSubst)
			self.noteClonedDefs(cloned)
			self.module.spans.current = calleeSpan
			self.module.appendOp(fresh, op: cloned)

			if aborts 'nothingAfterItRuns'
				return true
			end 'nothingAfterItRuns'
		end 'eachOp'

		return false
	end 'copyBlockOps'

	function noteClonedDefs(cloned StdOp)
		stdOpDefinedValue(cloned, def: self.def)
		self.highestDefined = self.def.maxDefinedId(self.highestDefined)
	end 'noteClonedDefs'

	// ⭐ **A `ret` BECOMES A BRANCH TO THE CONTINUATION carrying what it was returning; every other
	// terminator is cloned with its block targets remapped.** A callee with two returns therefore gives
	// the continuation two incoming edges into one block arg, which is precisely what a phi is for.
	function copyBlockExit(calleeBlock IrBlock, freshRef BlockRef, contRef BlockRef, subst ValueIdArray, blocks SpliceBlocks, calleeFunc IrFunction)
		if not calleeBlock.hasTerminatorOp() 'oplessTerminator'
			self.carryOplessExit(calleeBlock, freshRef: freshRef, calleeFunc: calleeFunc)
			return
		end 'oplessTerminator'

		let term = self.module.getOp(calleeBlock.terminatorIndex())

		// The cloned exit is the callee's terminator, so it answers to the callee's own line — the body
		// walk left the cursor on this block's LAST op, which is a different statement.
		self.module.spans.current = self.module.spans.at(calleeBlock.terminatorIndex())

		match term 'exit'
			const or
				binOp or
				unaryOp or
				cmp or
				binOpImm or
				cmpImm or
				div or
				mod then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			condBranch or
				branch then self.cloneExit(calleeBlock, freshRef: freshRef, term: term, subst: subst, blocks: blocks)
			ret(retValId) then self.returnToContinuation(freshRef, contRef: contRef, argIds: self.returnedValues(calleeFunc, valueId: clonedValue(retValId, subst: subst)))
			param or
				call then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			retVoid then self.returnToContinuation(freshRef, contRef: contRef, argIds: ValueIdArray.create())
			globalAddr or
				loadIndirect or
				storeIndirect or
				osAllocPages or
				osWriteStdout or
				rdataAddr or
				tryCall or
				errorReturn or
				osExit or
				funcAddr or
				callIndirect or
				osPanic or
				osReadClock or
				osProcessSpawn or
				osProcessExitCode or
				osWaitHandles or
				osCloseHandle or
				osWaitHandle or
				osFreePages then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			switch then self.cloneExit(calleeBlock, freshRef: freshRef, term: term, subst: subst, blocks: blocks)
			witnessCall or
				osPipeCreate or
				osIocpCreate or
				osEventCreate or
				osThreadCreate or
				osLockInit or
				osLockEnter or
				osLockLeave or
				osReadOverlapped or
				osLastError or
				osProcessSpawnInherit or
				osCancelIo or
				osGetPid or
				osPipeCreateOutbound or
				osWriteFile or
				osReadWallClock or
				witnessTryCall or
				osFileOpenRead or
				osFileClose or
				osReadFile or
				osFileOpenWrite or
				osFileSize or
				osFileDelete or
				osFileRename or
				osFileFindFirst or
				osFileFindClose or
				osFileFindNext or
				osDirCreate or
				osDirCurrentPath or
				osFileAttributes or
				osPanicValue or
				osCommandLine or
				osStdHandle or
				osExecutablePath or
				osWriteStderr or
				stackRecordAddr or
				osOpenNamedSegment or
				osMapNamedSegment or
				osTickCountMs or
				osHandleSetInherit or
				osPipePeek or
				osEnvRead or
				osProcessTerminate or
				osSocketStartup or
				osSocketResolve or
				osSocketFreeAddrInfo or
				osSocketCreate or
				osSocketConnect or
				osSocketSend or
				osSocketRecv or
				osSocketClose or
				osCpuCount or
				osThreadCpuTicks or
				osDecommitPages or
				memFill or
				osCommitPages or
				osReservePages or
				atomicCas or
				atomicRmw or
				currentThreadToken or
				tlsAlloc or
				tlsSetValue or
				tlsSlotLoad or
				osSetEvent or
				osEnterBackgroundPriority then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			osFileOpenWriteExecutable then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			osEnvBlock or
				osEnvBlockFree then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			loadAcquire or
				storeRelease then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			osHandleFileType then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
			osCreateNamedSegment or
				osUnmapNamedSegment or
				osUnpublishNamedSegment or
				covPoint or
				osHostSample or
				osNetpoll then refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc)
		end 'exit'
	end 'copyBlockExit'

	// ⚠ **A BLOCK CAN HAVE NO TERMINATOR OP AT ALL, AND THAT IS NOT A MALFORMED BLOCK.** The parser marks
	// the continuation after a fully-terminated `if`/`else` — or after a `match` whose every arm breaks
	// or panics — `Terminator.dead`: unreachable, and therefore terminated by DECLARATION rather than by
	// an op (`IrBlock.Terminator`). Copying such a block means copying the SLOT, which is exactly what
	// `LowerMaxonToStd.carryOplessTerminator` does at the tier boundary and for the same reason —
	// `IrModule.assertAllBlocksTerminated` demands every block commit to one of the four states.
	//
	// ⭐ **THE BLOCK IS COPIED RATHER THAN DROPPED**: if a copied block's terminator names it, dropping it
	// would leave that branch pointing at a block id the column never mapped. It costs the caller a few
	// unreachable ops. `match-statements.break-only-arms-with-panic-arm` pins the shape
	// (`Holder.assertUsable`'s `afterbreak`).
	function carryOplessExit(calleeBlock IrBlock, freshRef BlockRef, calleeFunc IrFunction)
		var fresh = self.blockAt(freshRef)

		match calleeBlock.terminator 'carry'
			unset then panic("SiteSplicer.carryOplessExit: block '{String.from(calleeBlock.label)}' of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' is still `Terminator.unset` — `assertAllBlocksTerminated` refuses that at the end of the parse")
			dead then fresh.terminator = Terminator.dead
			fallthrough then panic("SiteSplicer.carryOplessExit: block '{String.from(calleeBlock.label)}' of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' is marked `fallthrough`, which is a TARGET-tier layout decision — no Std block carries one")
			opIndex then panic("SiteSplicer.carryOplessExit: block '{String.from(calleeBlock.label)}' of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' names a terminator op, which `hasTerminatorOp` has already excluded")
		end 'carry'
	end 'carryOplessExit'

	// A terminator that stays inside the copied body: `branch`, `condBranch` and `switch`.
	//
	// ⚠ The callee block's OWN outgoing edges are rebuilt as a FRESH array — never mutated in place, per
	// `IrBlock`'s phi-model rule: the three tiers share one copy of that model and it reaches back into
	// the parse memo, so an in-place write corrupts every later compile of the project with nothing
	// downstream that re-reads it to notice.
	function cloneExit(calleeBlock IrBlock, freshRef BlockRef, term StdOp, subst ValueIdArray, blocks SpliceBlocks)
		let cloned = self.cloner.clone(term, valueSubst: subst, blockSubst: blocks.blockSubst)
		self.module.setTerminator(self.blockAt(freshRef), op: cloned)

		var edges = BranchEdgeArray.create()

		for edge in calleeBlock.branchEdges 'eachEdge'
			var argIds = ValueIdArray.create()

			for argId in edge.argIds 'eachArg'
				argIds.push(clonedValue(argId, subst: subst))
			end 'eachArg'

			edges.push(BranchEdge.create(blocks.targetOf(edge.targetBlockId), argIds: argIds))
		end 'eachEdge'

		var fresh = self.blockAt(freshRef)
		fresh.branchEdges = edges
	end 'cloneExit'

	function returnToContinuation(freshRef BlockRef, contRef BlockRef, argIds ValueIdArray)
		let contId = self.blockAt(contRef).id
		self.module.setTerminator(self.blockAt(freshRef), op: StdOp.branch(contId))
		attachEdge(self.blockAt(freshRef), targetId: contId, argIds: argIds)
	end 'returnToContinuation'

	// --- block/op access ---

	function blockAt(blockRef BlockRef) returns IrBlock
		return try self.module.blocks.get(blockRef) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer: blockRef {blockRef} of '{String.from(self.func.name)}' is not in module.blocks")
	end 'blockAt'

	// The flat `module.ops` index a block names at `pos`. Read through a function for `IrModule.getOp`'s
	// stated reason: an inline `self.blockAt(...).opRefs.get(...)` borrows `module` across the append
	// that follows, which the self-compile refuses.
	function blockOpRefAt(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos) returns OpIndex
		return try self.blockAt(blockRef).opRefs.get(pos) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer: block {blockRef} of '{String.from(self.func.name)}' has no op at position {pos}")
	end 'blockOpRefAt'

	function opAt(blockRef BlockRef, pos BlockOpPos) returns StdOp
		return self.module.getOp(self.blockOpRefAt(blockRef, pos: pos))
	end 'opAt'

	// A copied block's source footprint. Read and written in ONE call for `IrModule.getOp`'s reason: the read
	// borrows `module` and the write mutates it.
	function carryBlockSourceInfo(sourceRef BlockRef, to BlockRef)
		let sourceInfo = try self.module.blockSourceInfo.get(sourceRef) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.carryBlockSourceInfo: blockSourceInfo has no entry for blockRef {sourceRef}")
		try self.module.blockSourceInfo.set(to, value: sourceInfo) otherwise panic("SiteSplicer.carryBlockSourceInfo: blockSourceInfo.set OOB at {to}")
	end 'carryBlockSourceInfo'
end 'SiteSplicer'

// `site` into `plan` at its place in (block position, op position) order — a handful of sites per caller
// per wave, so an insertion is the whole sort.
function insertByCoordinates(plan SiteAcrossArray, site SiteAcross)
	var at = plan.count()

	while at > 0 and precedesSite(site, other: plannedSiteAt(plan, index: at - 1)) 'findThePlace'
		at = at - 1
	end 'findThePlace'

	plan.insert(at, value: site)
end 'insertByCoordinates'

function precedesSite(site SiteAcross, other SiteAcross) returns bool
	return site.position < other.position or (site.position == other.position and site.opPos < other.opPos)
end 'precedesSite'

function plannedSiteAt(plan SiteAcrossArray, index SiteCount) returns SiteAcross
	return try plan.get(index) otherwise panic("plannedSiteAt: plan.get OOB at {index} against {plan.count()} planned site(s)")
end 'plannedSiteAt'

// ⭐ **THE STD-ABI CONVENTION, CHECKED WHERE IT IS SPENT.** `buildValueColumn` maps ids
// `0..paramCount-1` straight onto the call's arguments instead of scanning for `param` ops, which is
// right exactly while a `param` op's result id IS its index (`LowerMaxonToStd.emitParams` emits
// `StdOp.param(i as ValueId, index: i, …)`, and `InsertRangeChecks.rangedParamValue` rests on the same
// identity). A body that broke it would splice silently, reading the caller's same-numbered values.
function assertParamOpMatchesConvention(op StdOp, calleeFunc IrFunction)
	match op 'p'
		const or
			binOp or
			unaryOp or
			cmp or
			binOpImm or
			cmpImm or
			div or
			mod or
			condBranch or
			branch or
			ret then refuseNotAParamOp()
		param(resultId, index, _) then requireParamValueIsItsIndex(resultId, index: index, calleeFunc: calleeFunc)
		call or
			retVoid or
			globalAddr or
			loadIndirect or
			storeIndirect or
			osAllocPages or
			osWriteStdout or
			rdataAddr or
			tryCall or
			errorReturn or
			osExit or
			funcAddr or
			callIndirect or
			osPanic or
			osReadClock or
			osProcessSpawn or
			osProcessExitCode or
			osWaitHandles or
			osCloseHandle or
			osWaitHandle or
			osFreePages or
			switch or
			witnessCall or
			osPipeCreate or
			osIocpCreate or
			osEventCreate or
			osThreadCreate or
			osLockInit or
			osLockEnter or
			osLockLeave or
			osReadOverlapped or
			osLastError or
			osProcessSpawnInherit or
			osCancelIo or
			osGetPid or
			osPipeCreateOutbound or
			osWriteFile or
			osReadWallClock or
			witnessTryCall or
			osFileOpenRead or
			osFileClose or
			osReadFile or
			osFileOpenWrite or
			osFileSize or
			osFileDelete or
			osFileRename or
			osFileFindFirst or
			osFileFindClose or
			osFileFindNext or
			osDirCreate or
			osDirCurrentPath or
			osFileAttributes or
			osPanicValue or
			osCommandLine or
			osStdHandle or
			osExecutablePath or
			osWriteStderr or
			stackRecordAddr or
			osOpenNamedSegment or
			osMapNamedSegment or
			osTickCountMs or
			osHandleSetInherit or
			osPipePeek or
			osEnvRead or
			osProcessTerminate or
			osSocketStartup or
			osSocketResolve or
			osSocketFreeAddrInfo or
			osSocketCreate or
			osSocketConnect or
			osSocketSend or
			osSocketRecv or
			osSocketClose or
			osCpuCount or
			osThreadCpuTicks or
			osDecommitPages or
			memFill or
			osCommitPages or
			osReservePages or
			atomicCas or
			atomicRmw or
			currentThreadToken or
			tlsAlloc or
			tlsSetValue or
			tlsSlotLoad or
			osSetEvent or
			osEnterBackgroundPriority then refuseNotAParamOp()
		osFileOpenWriteExecutable then refuseNotAParamOp()
		osEnvBlock or
			osEnvBlockFree then refuseNotAParamOp()
		loadAcquire or
			storeRelease then refuseNotAParamOp()
		osHandleFileType then refuseNotAParamOp()
		osCreateNamedSegment or
			osUnmapNamedSegment or
			osUnpublishNamedSegment or
			covPoint or
			osHostSample or
			osNetpoll then refuseNotAParamOp()
	end 'p'
end 'assertParamOpMatchesConvention'

// The backstop every non-terminator arm of `copyBlockExit` shares — ONE string, for `notTheSplitCall`'s
// reason. Which op it was is not worth naming: any of them in a terminator slot is the same broken
// block, and `scanSpliceableShape` has already refused every op a body may not hold.
function refuseNotATerminator(calleeFunc IrFunction)
	panic("SiteSplicer.copyBlockExit: an op that is not a terminator is in the terminator slot of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}'")
end 'refuseNotATerminator'

// The backstop the two non-`param` arms share — ONE string, for `notTheSplitCall`'s reason.
function refuseNotAParamOp()
	panic("assertParamOpMatchesConvention: asked of an op that is not a `param` — its one caller has already classified it as one through `inlineOpRole`")
end 'refuseNotAParamOp'

function requireParamValueIsItsIndex(resultId ValueId, index ParamCount, calleeFunc IrFunction)
	if resultId != valueIdAtParamPosition(index) 'conventionBroken'
		panic("SiteSplicer: parameter {index} of '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' defines value {resultId} — the Std ABI reserves ids 0..paramCount-1 for the parameters IN ORDER, and the splice's value column maps them onto the call's arguments by that identity")
	end 'conventionBroken'

	if index >= calleeFunc.paramCount 'pastTheDeclaration'
		panic("SiteSplicer: '{String.from(calleeFunc.name)}' has a `param` op for position {index} but declares {calleeFunc.paramCount} parameter(s)")
	end 'pastTheDeclaration'
end 'requireParamValueIsItsIndex'
